  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Error Cause**\n\nI\'m currently trying to understand why I\'m getting a `auth/network-request-failed` error, even though Identity Toolkit is enabled and responding with a 400 MISSING_IDENTIFIER instead of the expected 403 API NOT ENABLED. The user\'s error message points toward Google Drive sign-in issues within Firebase, and I need to establish the connection.\n\n\n**Analyzing Error Context**\n\nI\'ve examined the full error stack from Firebase, seeing the failure occurs during the sign-in process with a credential. The error handling and fetch operations seem involved in the process, which is failing with `auth/network-request-failed`. Now, I\'ll attempt to pinpoint where the connection to the network is not working as intended.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Failure Root**\n\nI\'m now tracing the fetch operation: `_performFetchWithErrorHandling` calls `_performSignInRequest`, which calls `_signInWithCredential`, eventually leading to `PopupOperation.onAuthEvent`. It looks like the issue arises after the user authenticates via a popup and the parent window fetches `POST https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/v1/accounts:signInWithIdp`. This request, triggered by `_signInWithCredential`, is failing in the browser, likely due to an ad blocker or blocked third-party cookies.\n\n\n**Confirming Domain Restriction**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my suspicion: the Cloud Run origin (`ais-dev-p55zubkq37ae52rlsf5zht-920363950371.asia-east1.run.app`) isn\'t listed as an authorized domain in the Firebase Auth console. This, along with potential CORS issues, is the likely culprit. My working hypothesis is that when an unauthorized domain calls `signInWithIdp`, a \"domain not authorized\" or CORS error occurs and translates to an \"auth/network-request-failed\" error. I am now looking at the workspace integration skill and firebase configuration.\n\n\n**Proposing Token Flow Solution**\n\nI\'m now considering an alternative: using the Google Identity Services (GIS) token flow, which circumvents Firebase Identity Toolkit entirely. This involves the `google.accounts.oauth2.initTokenClient` and direct calls to `accounts.google.com`. The process will obtain the OAuth access token directly, bypassing the need for domain checks and eliminating the `auth/network-request-failed` error. Then, I can make calls to  `https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo` to get the user\'s profile information!\n\n\n**Exploring Token Flow Adaptations**\n\nI\'m now incorporating access to Google Drive\'s API directly via the GIS token. I\'ll be using `https://www.googleapis.com/drive/v3/files` and attaching the `access_token` for authorization. A fallback to Firebase Auth is in place should GIS fail, and a manual token or share link input option will be available as a last resort. I\'ve also confirmed that I can include `<script src=\"https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client\" async defer></script>` in `index.html` to load the official GIS library. Finally, I\'ve confirmed that I know the OAuth client ID.\n\n\n**Integrating Token Client**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal location to initialize the GIS token client; `src/lib/googleDrive.ts` seems like a good fit. I\'ve mapped out the `google.accounts.oauth2.initTokenClient` flow. When `signInWithGoogleDrive()` is called, it first attempts the GIS token client. I have to make sure to handle errors from `tokenClient.requestAccessToken` and, if it succeeds, to fetch user info from `https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo` using the obtained access token.\n\n\n**Addressing Script Loading**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a potential edge case: what happens if the `window.google` object isn\'t yet available when I need it? To address this, I\'m implementing a dynamic script injection method to ensure the Google Identity Services (GIS) client library is loaded before I attempt to use it. This will prevent race conditions and ensure that the library is always available when needed.\n\n\n**Refining Sign-In Strategy**\n\nI\'m revisiting the sign-in flow logic in `src/lib/googleDrive.ts` to improve error handling and fallback mechanisms. I\'m focusing on clarifying how the GIS token client and Firebase Auth interact when either one, or both, encounters issues. My aim is to make the process more resilient to network failures or blocked services.\n\n\n"
