    thinking: "**Considering Theme Options**\n\nI\'m currently focused on expanding the theme selection within `UniversalModeBar.tsx`. The aim is to introduce a system-based auto-theme option, maybe replacing existing buttons. I\'m exploring an `\354\236\220\353\217\231 (\354\213\234\354\212\244\355\205\234)` or `\354\213\234\354\212\244\355\205\234` button, or potentially indicating `\354\236\220\353\217\231` when `isSystemTheme` is active. I believe it\'s elegant and functional.\n\n\n**Implementing System Theme**\n\nI\'ve decided to implement a `\'system\'` theme mode, which will automatically adapt to the user\'s OS color scheme using `prefers-color-scheme`. This will render the UI as either `dark` or `white` based on system preferences. I\'m also ensuring the app re-renders instantly upon system theme changes by syncing `document.documentElement` with the `effectiveTheme`. The user can tap `\354\213\234\354\212\244\355\205\234`, `\353\260\261\354\247\200`, `\353\213\244\355\201\254`, `\354\262\255\354\202\254\354\247\204` directly in the mode bar to switch themes.\n\n\n**Addressing Long Lines**\n\nI\'m now diving into fixing the long lines issue. I\'m starting with `src/lib/libreDwgLoader.ts`, and I\'m adding comprehensive checks for invisible entities, PaperSpace entities, and non-printable layers to filter them out during processing. I\'m focusing on the logic within the `libreDwgLoader` to prevent these elements from causing display issues. I\'ll meticulously update the code in `dxfParser.ts` and `modelExpander.ts` next, after which I plan to conduct extensive unit testing.\n\n\n**Refining Entity Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining how specific entities are handled in the parsing process. Specifically, I\'m adjusting how I manage `DIMENSION` entities, ensuring their insertion point is correctly set to (0, 0) as expected in the AutoCAD/DWG context. I\'m also adding logic to prevent the rendering of certain frame types like `XLINE`, `RAY`, `VIEWPORT`, and `WIPEOUT` since they are unnecessary. Finally, I\'m implementing a check for DXF code 60 within `dxfParser.ts` as an additional measure.\n\n\n**Filtering Unwanted Entities**\n\nI\'m now incorporating additional filtering logic. I\'m focusing on skipping the rendering of invisible entities, as well as those in paper space, by checking DXF codes 60 and 67. I\'m also ensuring that DIMENSION entities are initialized with a position of (0, 0), as expected. Finally, I\'m hiding DEFPOINTS layers by default, to ensure only printable entities are shown.\n\n\n**Analyzing Line Behavior**\n\nI\'m now investigating how to interpret lines that span large portions of the drawing. I\'m focusing on identifying criteria to differentiate meaningful lines, such as title block borders, from potential artifacts or infinite rays. I\'m considering using thresholds like 45% of `spanH`, `spanW`, or 35% of `maxDiag` as potential indicators. Analyzing line length relative to drawing extents, especially for lines on layers such as `DIM`, `OBJ`, and `CONSTRUCTION`, is key. I am checking polyline closure to address potential connection errors.\n\n\n**Refining Line Processing**\n\nI\'m now implementing heuristics to distinguish meaningful lines from potential artifacts, focusing on polyline closure and segment length anomalies. I\'m setting a check, such as if the distance between the last and first vertices is greater than four times the median segment length, with a threshold based on the maximum diagonal, I will mark the polyline as open. For long segments in LWPOLYLINE, I\'ll identify and split the polyline. Additionally, I\'m filtering out streak lines and ensuring that any elements from the `DEFPOINTS` layer are omitted.\n\n\n"
