  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Comparing Visual Representations**\n\nI am now thoroughly examining the mobile app and AutoCAD views, specifically `image_51cfee32.png` and `image_293c8b98.png`. I\'m comparing them closely to identify key differences and similarities in the visual representations. I\'m focusing on how the information is presented in each setting.\n\n\n**Analyzing Line Origins**\n\nI\'m now investigating that anomalous diagonal line appearing in the mobile app view, `image_51cfee32.png`. It\'s absent in the AutoCAD drawing, `image_293c8b98.png`. I\'m exploring its origin\342\200\224is it a leader, block insertion, polyline, or something else entirely? The AutoCAD drawing shows no such line connecting the conveyor belt\'s arrows to the control tower. Why the discrepancy?\n\n\n**Pinpointing Line Characteristics**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! I\'ve determined that the diagonal line in `image_51cfee32.png` starts at the top conveyor and ends at the right machine tower. The tower end has an arrow/bubble, pointing towards a possible `LEADER` or `MULTILEADER`. I\'m also now considering a polyline with vertices within the conveyor and at the tower, forming the diagonal. There\'s a parallel line inside the conveyor, suggesting a possible single polyline connecting the conveyor and the tower. I\'m noting the AutoCAD representation (`image_293c8b98.png`) shows yellow/red lines in the tower.\n\n\n**Identifying Omissions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on what\'s absent in the mobile view, specifically compared to the AutoCAD drawing. The most glaring omission is the large yellow outer border frame, complete with grid markers, which is completely missing in the mobile app\'s representation. I\'m recalling the code in `modelExpander.ts` and the `isOrthogonal` check. It seems the code filters out these orthogonal lines, which explains why the frame is not displayed.\n\n\n**Detecting Filter Failures**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue! It seems the `isArtifactStreakSegment` filter, used to remove \"artifact streaks,\" is accidentally eliminating legitimate long lines. These include the crucial green dimension lines that I\'ve been comparing in `image_51cfee32.png` and `image_293c8b98.png`. Specifically, the code\'s length check ( `len > Math.max(spanW, spanH) * 0.35`) is the culprit! This unintended consequence is causing important details, like dimension arrows and text, to be rendered incorrectly or omitted entirely in the mobile app. I\'m now zeroing in on dimension entities to figure this out!\n\n\n**Investigating Dimension Origin**\n\nI\'m now diving into how dimension entities are handled, especially looking at the potential origin point. I\'m focusing on why a dimension block might exist at (0, 0) in the DWG, or have an insertion point there, and exploring if this is always the case. I\'m checking `processBlockRecord` in `libreDwgLoader.ts` and the `basePoint` and `origin` properties.\n\n\n**Locating Displacement Causes**\n\nI\'ve just had a major breakthrough regarding the missing dimensions! It seems when `ins` (insertion point) is `{ x: 0, y: 0 }`, and `bp` (base point) is the block definition point (like the (42000, 9000) of a dimension block), then a translation happens. This shifts dimension entities massively. I\'m seeing `-42000` causing all dimension lines to move completely off-screen. This explains their disappearance! The conveyor line is a block, just like the dimension.\n\n\n"
