    thinking: "**Analyzing the visuals**\n\nI\'m deep-diving into the visual data. Right now, I\'m meticulously comparing `image_51cfee32.png` (app view) against `image_293c8b98.png` (desktop AutoCAD). My attention is focused on the outer border in AutoCAD; specifically, the large yellow rectangle `A-5` that acts as the drawing\'s outer frame. The inner grid lines and associated lettering are also under scrutiny.\n\n\n**Investigating the Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the title block and the missing yellow border. I see the title block\'s lower portion in the app view, complete with magenta lines, confirming the presence of this element. The absence of the yellow border, however, is a glaring omission. I\'m digging into `modelExpander.ts` to see if a filter is inadvertently excluding the border elements, particularly focusing on the `isBorderLayer` check and related conditions. The aim is to understand why this crucial frame isn\'t rendering.\n\n\n**Revisiting Boundary Conditions**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering the boundary calculations, especially how `spanW` and `spanH` are determined. The use of sorted samples (`xSamples`, `ySamples`) with a trimmed range is raising a red flag. What happens if the majority of the points cluster within the drawing, as is typical, and the border only has a few key points? The current approach might be discarding essential boundary points. This could be why the yellow rectangle is missing.\n\n\n**Examining Layer Filters**\n\nI\'m now deeply concerned about the layer filtering logic. It appears that specific layer names and line lengths are being used to identify and exclude potential artifacts, but the criteria seem overly aggressive. Specifically, I\'m worried about the impact of filtering lines based on layer IDs `0`, `1`, `7`, or `CONT`, especially when combined with the length threshold. This approach could inadvertently remove critical elements like the outer border, title block lines, and inner grid lines, all of which might be on layer `0` (or `YELLOW` in AutoCAD). The current method might be too restrictive, and I need to assess how these filtering conditions interplay.\n\n\n**Uncovering Hidden Causes**\n\nI\'m now investigating the origins of the filtering, especially in light of the user\'s prompt about the unwanted long lines. Prompt 8 and the attached screenshot show unwanted blue/cyan horizontal/vertical lines that shouldn\'t be visible. I\'m focusing on the condition \"Long horizontal or vertical line cutting through interior\" and its implementation in `src/lib/libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically, the checks for `raw.invisible === 1` and `raw.in`. The goal is to understand if these conditions are correctly filtering out these phantom elements, and if not, how to refine the filtering to eliminate these problems.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Root**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `processBlockRecord` and the handling of dimension entities. Specifically, the processing of dimension block records is drawing my attention. I think I\'m seeing a possible root cause in how block base points and insertion points for dimensions are handled, potentially causing visual discrepancies, like the overlapping lines.\n\n\n**Identifying the Reset**\n\nI\'ve just spotted something critical! In `libreDwgLoader.ts`, line 564 sets `ent.position` to `{ x: 0, y: 0 }`. This resets the entity\'s position to the origin. This conflicts directly with the insertion point handling in `modelExpander.ts`, specifically lines 145-157, which calculates a new insertion point based on the block\'s base point and the insert entity\'s position. This reset to the origin may explain the rendering issues. I\'m following the logic to see where and how this `0,0` assignment is affecting element positioning.\n\n\n**Calculating the Displacement**\n\nI\'ve just done the math and identified a significant translation error. Specifically, I calculated the impact of the insertion point being (0,0) and the block base point being (42659, 9654). Every entity within the `DIMENSION` block `*D...` gets shifted by (-42659, -9654)! I have a solid hypothesis, that all dimension elements are then offset into outer space. The previous streak was a consequence of this. Now I will analyze the DWG and the block\'s basepoint.\n\n\n**Verifying Block Transformations**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on the implications of dimension blocks. I\'ve confirmed that entities within anonymous dimension blocks like `*D...` are drawn in WCS coordinates. This means the block\'s `basePoint` dictates the insertion. The crucial detail I\'m currently verifying is whether `libreDwgLoader.ts` is correctly handling this WCS geometry, and what the correct transformation should be for elements like `basePoint`. I need to ensure the transformation aligns with AutoCAD\'s approach.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Color Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the dimension color mismatch. The AutoCAD image clearly shows green dimensions, while the app renders none, just green parts of the machine blocks. I\'m focusing on why the color is lost and the transformation error. The core problem remains the displacement caused by the origin reset in `libreDwgLoader.ts`, and I\'m analyzing the color handling within the blocks now.\n\n\n**Examining Offset Errors**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implications of `blk.basePoint` being erroneously set to (0, 0) for dimension blocks. With this origin reset, every entity inside dimension blocks will be rendered at its WCS coordinates, which might explain the dimension placement issues. I\'m investigating how this affects line positions and dimensions within `*D...` blocks, and also looking at the code to confirm if all dimensions are being drawn at their exact WCS coordinates.\n\n\n\n**Connecting the Components**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the diagonal line connecting the top machine to the yellow tower in `image_51cfee32.png`. It is light gray. I\'m focusing on why a diagonal line connects these elements, considering the presence of the Korean text and leader arrow in the AutoCAD image. The color of the line, ACI 7 (white) is significant and warrants more research. I\'m also comparing this to data from prompt 8\'s screenshot.\n\n\n\n"
