    thinking: "**Analyzing Insertion Point Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the insertion point logic, specifically lines 2097-2102. I see how the code handles `insertionPoint` and `clonePoint`, using either as a basis, and defaults to {x: 0, y: 0} if none are available. I\'m noting the `ocs.pt` function\'s role in this stage, checking the validity of insertion point coordinates. I want to understand how this point is ultimately used.\n\n\n**Investigating Coordinate Transformations**\n\nI\'m now investigating coordinate transformations related to dimension entities. I see that the `insertionPoint` in the raw data corresponds to the dimension\'s location. This is important to note, because the `basePoint` of the anonymous block record holds the block\'s base point. I\'m focusing on why a previous modification set the entity\'s position to {x: 0, y: 0} and the effects on the subsequent calculations, and how this affects the tx,ty translation factors. It\'s becoming clearer how these points interact, especially when the insertion point is equal to the base point, resulting in translation being effectively zeroed out.\n\n\n**Examining Dimension Block Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on anonymous dimension blocks, specifically those starting with `*D`. I see that the base point in LibreDWG, if non-zero, combined with a zero or missing insertion point, creates discrepancies. My investigation points to two scenarios in AutoCAD: either entities in these blocks use absolute WCS coordinates, which necessitates zero translation, or they\'re relative to (0,0) and placed by the insertion point. I\'m focusing on validating which approach is present, to determine the correct transformation.\n\n\n**Confirming WCS Coordinate Behavior**\n\nNow I\'ve found definitive confirmation: AutoCAD\'s dimension blocks use WCS coordinates. The key is in those \'*D...\' anonymous blocks.  This is important as it explains the discrepancies I\'ve seen. AutoCAD sets the base and insertion points at either the origin or the defpoint. If the insertion point differs from the base point, or is missing entirely, zero translation effectively places entities directly in WCS. The key now is tracking basePoint and entity position to correctly interpret the transformations. I need to validate how those are set.\n\n\n**Validating Coordinate Zeroing**\n\nI\'ve confirmed a critical behavior: for all dimension blocks starting with `*D...`, both the block\'s `basePoint` and the dimension entity\'s `position` are set to `{ x: 0, y: 0 }`. This means that any entity contained within that block will be positioned at its precise WCS coordinates. This leads to `tx` and `ty` translation factors being equal to 0, and no scaling or rotation, regardless of a non-zero raw insertion point. I\'m now checking this for dimension entities.\n\n\n**Correcting Basepoint Assignments**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical discrepancy. The code wasn\'t correctly zeroing the `basePoint` for dimension blocks (`*D...`) as it should, as indicated in `libreDwgLoader.ts`, line 665. Now I will make the relevant correction to adhere to the standard WCS coordinate behavior. Specifically, the base point, as well as the dimension entity position, MUST be {x: 0, y: 0} in this scenario.\n\n\n**Verifying Dimension Entity Location**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the core problem: Incorrect base point assignment caused significant dimension displacement. Previously, I was incorrectly calculating translation factors because the base point wasn\'t zeroed for dimension blocks, resulting in all dimension elements shifting. Now, with the code correctly setting the `basePoint` and `ent.position` to `{x: 0, y: 0}` within the dimension blocks, I\'m confident the calculations will be accurate, and the visual issues in the drawing will be resolved, because the correct zero translation will be in place.\n\n\n**Investigating Border Visibility**\n\nI\'m now investigating the disappearance of the outer yellow border and grid markings. I\'m focusing on the images `image_51cfee32.png` and `image_293c8b98.png` to find discrepancies in the rendering. The key difference is the outer yellow rectangle, the paper frame and grid numbers which are missing in the new image. I need to pinpoint why these entities are no longer being rendered, and check their location in the DWG file. The scale values are set and validated, as the scale factors for dimension entities are consistently set to 1.\n\n\n**Determining PaperSpace Flag**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visibility issue of the yellow border. I\'ve been considering why the outer border and grid marks are missing in the new image. Based on observations in AutoCAD, I\'ve confirmed the drawing is in Model Space, indicated by the active tab being `[\353\252\250\355\230\225]`. This leads me to focus on the `in_paperspace` flag in `libreDwgLoader.ts` and the hypothesis that this flag might be erroneously set for entities within the title block. I need to validate if LibreDWG might be misinterpreting the title block\'s origin within PaperSpace. I\'m recalling how the blue title sheet border was visible at the bottom of a screenshot.\n\n\n**Analyzing Line Visibility**\n\nI\'m now investigating the appearance of long lines, specifically in `Screenshot_20261105_122714_Chrome_e38fb563.jpg`. I\'m noting the presence of blue lines at the bottom, and vertical blue lines. I\'m focusing on the code in `modelExpander.ts` lines 353-374, particularly the section intended to filter orthogonal lines. I am investigating the criteria used to identify and potentially suppress lines, especially those related to borders, titles, frames, and sheets. I will check how these conditions are applied to the identified blue lines.\n\n\n**Verifying Layer Zero Entities**\n\nI\'ve discovered the reason for the missing yellow border. The problematic filter in `modelExpander.ts` was suppressing lines on Layer 0. This layer is the one holding the yellow border lines, whose offset from the document\'s edge is greater than 3.5% of the overall width and height. I\'m verifying the fix.\n\n\n**Identifying Artifact Removal**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the artifact removal culprit: the filter in `modelExpander.ts` erroneously identified Layer 0 entities, which included the vital yellow border, as artifacts. This resulted in the removal of the outer yellow border, grid lines, and margin lines, along with any orthogonal dimension or alignment lines exceeding a size threshold. My focus now is on confirming the deletion criteria, which included lines from orthogonal layer 0, `DO`, `A-5`, `FORM`, or `\354\226\221\354\213\235` layer elements. I will also investigate the origin of the blue lines.\n\n\n**Examining Layer Visibility Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating layer visibility and its impact on entity rendering. I see that the filtering in `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically in the `mapRawEntity` function (lines 301-342), omits checking if a layer is turned off or frozen. The code only checks for `DEFPOINTS`, `NPLT`, `NO_PLOT`, and `NON_PLOT`. My focus is to determine how to incorporate the `l.off` and `l.frozen` layer properties into the entity visibility checks to handle entities on invisible layers correctly.\n\n\n**Correcting Layer Visibility Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on layer visibility, and I\'ve found a critical oversight. The code was not filtering out entities on hidden or turned-off layers, like DEFPOINTS, NPLT, NO_PLOT, and NON_PLOT, in `libreDwgLoader.ts`. Even when a layer was set to `off: true` or `frozen: true`, or had an invalid `colorIndex`, entities on those layers were still being processed and rendered. In `modelExpander.ts`, `isExcluded` was used for extents calculation only, not to filter entities, leading to the application rendering invisible entities! The correction will implement the correct filtering.\n\n\n**Validating Layer Visibility**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the elegant discovery: a crucial logic error was rendering entities from hidden layers. Now, I see the code never checked layer visibility during entity processing in `expandAndOptimizeDrawing`. Consequently, entities on frozen or turned-off layers, like the blue lines in `Screenshot_20261105_122714_Chrome_e38fb563.jpg`, were still rendered. The aggressive deletion attempted to remove the artifacts, unintentionally removing the border in `image_51cfee32.png`. The fix involves correctly filtering by checking layer visibility properties.\n\n\n**Tracing Diagonal Line Origin**\n\nI\'m now tracing the origin of a diagonal light gray/white line that appears in `image_51cfee32.png` but not in `image_293c8b98.png`. I\'ve determined it originates from the conveyor of the top machine, and ends precisely at the warning lamp atop the yellow tower on the bottom right. I see a white line inside the conveyor, and dimensions above the machine. I want to correlate this with missing entities in the model.\n\n\n**Investigating Polyline Types**\n\nI\'m now investigating the possible origins of the diagonal line, and I am exploring whether it is a `LEADER`, `POLYLINE`, or `LWPOLYLINE`. I\'m noting the presence of text and an arrow in `image_293c8b98.png`, indicating possible `LEADER` usage, pointing down. I\'ll focus on how `libreDwgLoader.ts` parses `LWPOLYLINE` and `POLYLINE` (lines 383-432) to correctly handle these types. I will see if I can correlate any entity vertex data with the line.\n\n\n**Analyzing Polyline Data**\n\nI\'m now analyzing how polylines are handled, focusing on closed vs. open states. I\'m checking flags and properties, especially for `LWPOLYLINE`, `POLYLINE` and `LEADER` types. The current logic determines if an entity is closed by checking raw data flags and properties, such as `shape`, `closed` or various bits. Specifically, I am cross-referencing this against the diagonal line. I\'ll need to check the vertex data for `vlist`, and determine if its points are valid.\n\n\n**Examining Polyline Closure Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical issue within the polyline handling, specifically related to the `closed` state determination. I see the code is misinterpreting the `raw.shape` flag in LibreDWG, potentially setting `closed` to true erroneously. The flag is related to shape attributes, not closure, and the bitwise check for 512 is also incorrect. This seems to be the cause of closed polylines being erroneously drawn as closed. I will correct the `closed` determination, focusing on the DWG specifications to ensure proper closure detection for polylines and LWPOLYLINES.\n\n\n**Correcting Polyline Closure Flag**\n\nI\'m now focused on rectifying the polyline closure logic in `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I\'ve identified an incorrect `closed` flag interpretation, specifically for `LWPOLYLINE`, due to a misinterpretation of `raw.shape` and an incorrect bitwise check. The correct check should be `rawFlag & 1`. This misinterpretation caused open polylines to be erroneously rendered as closed, resulting in unexpected line connections. I am revising the closure determination to accurately reflect polyline properties based on the DWG specification.\n\n\n**Tracing Diagonal Line Data**\n\nI\'m now tracing the origin of the diagonal line and related entities, focusing on the coordinates in `image_51cfee32.png`. It seems that the crosshair position is X: 42659, Y: 9654, which, given the 6% zoom and the coordinate overlay, I\'m checking to see if these coordinate values intersect with the diagonal line. I\'m focusing on validating the 4 view mechanical drawing structure, with Plan, Front, and Side views.\n\n\n\n**Tracing Projection Line Origin**\n\nNow I\'m tracing a specific diagonal line, and I hypothesize that this could be a projection line to connect the TOP and RIGHT SIDE views. These are often used for dimensions, and I must check if this line is a projection, guide, reference, or other construction line. It seems the line on the new model is on Layer `0` (zero). This is the base layer and its properties are critical.\n\n\n**Investigating Layer Visibility**\n\nI\'m now investigating layer visibility and I\'ve focused on `modelExpander.ts` and `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I noticed the omission of a visibility check for frozen or off layers. My plan is to include `layer.off` and `layer.frozen` properties into the rendering condition to ensure entities on invisible layers are correctly omitted from the expanded model. This will fix the missing artifacts!\n\n\n**Implementing Layer Visibility Filtering**\n\nI\'m now implementing layer visibility filtering to exclude entities on hidden layers, like DEFPOINTS. The current implementation in `modelExpander.ts` fails to check `layers[e.layer].visible` when adding entities to `finalEntities`. This leads to the rendering of all entities, even on hidden layers. I will introduce the missing checks in `modelExpander.ts` lines 378-460.\n\n\n"
