  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing LibreDWG Loader**\n\nI\'m currently investigating how `MLEADER` and `MULTILEADER` entities are handled within `libreDwgLoader.ts`. It seems they are not explicitly addressed, and thus fall into the default `null` return. Now I\'m focusing on the existing entity parsing logic within the loader, specifically examining how other entity types, like `LINE`, are currently managed. I\'ll need to understand the existing structure before I can determine the best approach for these new entity types.\n\n\n**Examining Entity Handling**\n\nI\'ve been looking deeper into the entity parsing within `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically how different entity types are handled in the `else if` statements. I\'m focusing now on `SOLID`, `3DFACE`, and `TRACE` and noted that `3DFACE` are handled as `LWPOLYLINE`. I\'m curious to see how this approach is being applied to these entities, specifically, how they affect `closed` and `vertices` properties.\n\n\n**Considering SOLID Entities**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how `SOLID` and `TRACE` entities might be used for arrowheads. It\'s becoming clear that `SOLID` entities represent arrowheads in AutoCAD, having three or four points. I\'m noting that the point order for these arrowheads is being handled as `pts = [pts[0], pts[1], pts[3], pts[2]]`. If the third point is coincident with the second, it forms a triangle. I\'m wondering if this method can be leveraged as an efficient method. I\'m focusing on why a perfectly straight line could have been constructed using a line segment.\n\n\n**Investigating LINE Origins**\n\nI\'m now diving into the purpose behind a `LINE` entity observed in the DWG files. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the coordinates (43000, 15000) and (49000, 9000). I\'m looking at two images, `image_51cfee32.png` and `image_293c8b98.png`. I\'m comparing the locations of machines, conveyor rollers, and a tower to derive the function of these lines. I think I\'ve found an alignment.\n\n\n**Exploring Projection Logic**\n\nI\'m now considering the principles of orthographic projection in mechanical drafting. I\'m focusing on how draftsmen create projections between top and side views, using guide lines and centerlines. I am considering where these lines are placed in the CAD software layer scheme, and noting that they might be on layers like `GUIDE` or `CONSTRUCTION`, or even a layer that is frozen or turned off. I\'m noting this layer visibility is important. I\'m now testing whether visibility is respected within our application.\n\n\n**Evaluating Layer Visibility**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the layer visibility logic within the code, trying to see how layer properties like `off`, `frozen`, and color index interact to determine layer visibility. I am observing the conditions for the `isOff` variable, and its implication on the visibility flag. I see it includes checks for layer names like \'DEFPOINTS\' and also takes the color index as a negative number to be off. It is interesting to see this approach to determining visible layers.\n\n\n\n**Pinpointing Visibility Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the `mapRawEntity` function handles entity visibility, and I\'ve found it checks various \"invisible\" flags. I\'m focusing on the `raw.invisible` property and its values in both LibreDWG and AutoCAD DWG. I\'ve confirmed that `raw.invisible === 1` indicates invisibility. I\'m also now pondering whether `mapRawEntity` considers layer properties, which I see it does not.\n\n\n**Uncovering Layer Filtering**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously tracing the layer visibility logic and it turns out `mapRawEntity` doesn\'t filter entities based on layer visibility. I then inspected `expandAndOptimizeDrawing` and it also doesn\'t consider layer visibility. However, I found a filtering step in `CADCanvas.tsx` that appears to handle layer visibility based on `batch.layer`, but if an entity within an `INSERT` block has a layer not defined in the model, the visibility check fails and the entity renders regardless!\n\n\n**Examining Layer Retrieval**\n\nI\'m now investigating a potential root cause for spurious lines, focusing on how layer information is retrieved from the LibreDWG database in `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I\'m checking the logic around `layerEntries`, specifically lines 245 to determine if layer data is correctly populated across different LibreDWG versions. If `layerEntries` is empty, all layers default to being visible! It\'s also possible that the diagonal line isn\'t a visibility issue, but a parsing artifact. I\'ll need to analyze if the spurious line\'s coordinates have any relation to the original drawing\'s entities.\n\n\n**Identifying Phantom Lines**\n\nI\'m now investigating a perplexing line in the AutoCAD drawing, specifically, the one connecting the conveyor\'s center to the tower\'s tip. I\'ve concluded it\'s a phantom, unlikely to be a deliberate design element. I\'ve listed possible reasons for its creation, focusing on cases where 3D-to-2D exports and DWG parsing might generate a polyline. I will now examine `POLYLINE2D`, `POLYLINE3D`, and `LWPOLYLINE` logic.\n\n\n**Analyzing Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'ve been looking at the `LWPOLYLINE` entity, specifically the `closed` property, within `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I noticed how the `raw.shape` flag, frequently present, forces `ent.closed` to be true. This, combined with `ctx.closePath()`, creates an unexpected line connecting the last and first vertex of the polyline. I need to understand this better.\n\n\n**Correcting Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'ve homed in on the phantom line: a closed polyline erroneously connecting the conveyor to the tower tip. The `closed` flag, incorrectly set by the loader, forces `ctx.closePath()`, creating the line. I\'ve found `raw.shape` is mistakenly used to determine if a polyline is closed, and that `(rawFlag & 1)` should determine closure. This matches AutoCAD DWG behavior. I will refactor.\n\n\n**Fixing Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the incorrect polyline closure, which caused phantom lines. The issue stems from the improper handling of the `closed` flag, specifically in `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I found `raw.shape` and `(rawFlag & 512)` were incorrectly treated as closure indicators. I\'ve corrected this by using `(rawFlag & 1)` and additional logic. This matches DXF parsing behavior and addresses the core problem. The fix should resolve the spurious diagonal lines in the drawing!\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the edge cases of polyline closure, particularly focusing on how disconnected segments and multiple contours within a single polyline entity are handled. I\'ve switched my attention to `modelExpander.ts` and noted a check for polyline closure that appears to address spurious connecting lines based on vertex distance. My primary focus is if `e.closed` is true and `e.vertices.length` is 2.\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the nuances of polyline closure, questioning the handling of 2-vertex closed polylines. Specifically, the condition `if (e.closed && e.vertices.length >= 3)` in `modelExpander.ts` is skipping 2-vertex polylines. I\'m exploring the implications of closing these and if a very long closing segment is drawn even if the points are far apart, and also why a 2-vertex polyline would even be *closed*. I\'m testing segment distance against median step for accurate closure. I\'m also considering the source of the diagonal line and whether it may not be a *closed* polyline but instead, an individual *LINE*.\n\n\n**Calculating Diagonal Artifacts**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the logic in `modelExpander.ts` to identify and remove diagonal artifacts. I\'m focusing on the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function, particularly the conditions used to determine if a line should be discarded. I\'m examining the `maxDiag` variable, which seems crucial, and attempting to calculate its value based on the drawing sheet size (A5), the machine conveyor width (1214mm), and the scaling factor. I\'m noting the ratio dx/dy. I\'m investigating why the `len > maxDiag * 0.20` check didn\'t catch the diagonal, focusing on the relationship between line length and the `maxDiag`.\n\n\n**Calculating Drawing Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now calculating the likely drawing coordinates based on machine and frame sizes in the DWG. I\'ve realized the drawing\'s world coordinates are around (40000, 10000), which explains the X and Y coordinates. I\'m noting that arbitrary placements are common for draftsmen, and a coordinate (0, 0) impacts span calculations. I\'ve determined that `maxDiag` is about 53,851 and 20% is approximately 10,770, informing my artifact removal strategy.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Removal**\n\nI\'m now refining the artifact removal strategy, calculating the line length to be around 5,830 and realizing my length check failed because the `maxDiag` value was inflated due to a coordinate origin issue. The legitimate length should never be this large! I\'m now establishing a much smaller maximum length, considering the scale of the drawing and typical mechanical elements like chamfers and bracing, to effectively remove these spurious diagonal lines. This also helps with span calculations.\n\n\n**Confirming Phantom Line Origin**\n\nI\'ve determined the diagonal line is definitively a phantom artifact, traversing the empty space between the plan and elevation views of the machines. The machine distances and the line\'s position confirm this. I\'ve also identified the dimensions in the drawing, noting the green color. Now I will use this data to calculate the line length relative to the overall drawing scale.\n\n\n**Investigating Dimension Layers**\n\nI\'m now investigating dimension layer handling, noting their absence in a specific image. I\'m focusing on why dimension entities might be returning null within `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically the `DIMENSION` block handling. I am noting the logic for dimension entities at line 551 within `libreDwgLoader.ts` and line 660-672 in `processBlockRecord` that may be causing this. I\'m analyzing block name processing and conditional returns.\n\n\n**Analyzing Dimension Rendering**\n\nI\'m now investigating how dimension entities are rendered, specifically focusing on the translation and base point logic in `libreDwgLoader.ts` and `modelExpander.ts`. I see dimension blocks are anonymous and translated based on their definition point, like `*D1`. I\'m noting the importance of `basePoint` to determine how the anonymous block\'s contents are translated and positioned on the drawing. This may impact how dimensions are being presented.\n\n\n**Uncovering Dimension Displacement**\n\nI\'ve just figured out that dimension entities with block names starting with `*D` are getting translated to (0,0)! The insertion point is being set as 0,0, which sets an insertion point to the origin! This means all dimension lines and text get their coordinates adjusted, which moves the green dimensioned lines on the drawing from their intended placement. The dimensions were displaced from their locations. It appears to be an unintended side effect.\n\n\n**Confirming Dimension Issues**\n\nI\'ve determined dimension entities with block names like `*D` are always inserted at (0,0), which displaces them. Both LibreDWG and DXF parsing set the base point to (0,0) for dimension blocks, leading to the origin translation. The green dimension lines appear correctly in their relative positions. This behavior appears consistent across formats, suggesting a design choice, but leading to the unwanted translation of dimension entities.\n\n\n**Addressing Dimension Positioning**\n\nI\'m now investigating dimension entity positioning. My focus is on ensuring dimensions aren\'t translated to the origin. It\'s become clear how dimensions are always being inserted at (0,0) by using a base point of (0,0), regardless of their original position. I\'m focusing on why this translation occurs, and I am exploring possible adjustments within `libreDwgLoader.ts`. I am considering how this issue manifests in the final visualization of the drawing.\n\n\n**Evaluating Artifact Removal Logic**\n\nI\'m now analyzing artifact removal logic, specifically in `isArtifactStreakSegment`, which targets lines not on border layers. My focus is on the `nearOuterX` and `nearOuterY` conditions, which trigger line removal if midpoints are within 3.5% of the drawing\'s outer boundary. This seems to be an aggressive removal condition for all lines which are not border lines. I\'m wondering if this overly aggressive condition removes too many segments.\n\n\n**Evaluating Artifact Removal**\n\nI\'m now focusing on why `isArtifactStreakSegment` was introduced to begin with. It seems this stems from the fact that hidden or invisible entities in the DWG drawing are being rendered. The user specifically stated these lines should be invisible and are being shown, and that our application is incorrectly rendering these hidden elements. I\'m noting this is related to unhandled layer visibility.\n\n\n**Examining Entity Invisibility**\n\nI\'m now investigating how invisibility is determined for entities. I\'ve found multiple flags, including `raw.invisible`, `raw.invis`, and `raw.is_invisible`. Additionally, a negative color index signifies an off layer, and entities on that layer should be hidden. PaperSpace also affects visibility. I need to determine where these flags are set for the current entities.\n\n\n**Revising Artifact Strategy**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering the criteria for artifact removal. The current approach, particularly the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function, inappropriately removes legitimate drawing elements, like the yellow border, because it focuses on line length and orthogonality. I\'m noting the drawing\'s *ModelSpace* context, and that layer `0` is always on. Orthogonal lines, sheet borders, and title block lines, should never be deleted. I will now remove the orthogonal line deletion entirely, to preserve critical drawing information.\n\n\n\n**Investigating Polyline Errors**\n\nI\'m now examining the cause of the phantom diagonal line, suspecting an improperly closed polyline. I\'m focusing on open polylines inadvertently closed by our loader, connecting conveyor belt paths with a tower. I\'m testing the `ent.closed` property within the code and testing for long closing segments in `modelExpander.ts`.\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical polyline closure scenario involving long, artifactual diagonal lines. I\'m focusing on the distance check between the last vertex and the first, as well as considering if these are LINE segments instead. If the distance or delta-x/y are large, I will set `closed = false`. I\'m testing this new logic, and focusing on a more reliable definition of when a line segment constitutes an artifact within a 2D mechanical blueprint.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Detection**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the drawing to refine artifact detection, specifically by analyzing `image_293c8b98.png`. I\'m focusing on the absence of long, legitimate diagonal lines; any diagonal exceeding 300 units is likely an artifact. The legitimate lines in the drawing are orthogonal, or short chamfers and arrowheads. Based on this observation, I will refine my artifact detection algorithm with a stricter length threshold and directionality check.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Detection**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into refining artifact detection. I\'ve been carefully reviewing the diagonal line\'s characteristics and its presence across images. It consistently appears as an artifact, with significant dx and dy values connecting disjointed parts. I\'m noting the drawing style, focusing on the absence of long, legitimate diagonals within the machinery layout. I will revise the artifact detection algorithm to be stricter based on these observations, focusing on segment length and directionality.\n\n\n**Analyzing Leader Entity Parsing**\n\nI\'m now investigating the parsing of the `LEADER` entity within `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically lines 388-432. I\'m noting the `isWcsPoly` flag and the coordinate extraction logic, which handles potential variations in point representation. I am now evaluating how this approach handles the specific properties of a `LEADER` entity, and focusing on whether or not it aligns with the intended representation.\n\n\n**Examining Leader Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating how `LEADER` entities are parsed, specifically examining `raw.points` and trying to understand point ordering and coordinate data. I\'m focusing on whether LibreDWG includes the annotation offset or block reference as the final point within `raw.points`. I see that LibreDWG can store annotation data and hook offsets, which is valuable.\n\n\n**Investigating Line Anomalies**\n\nI\'m now diving into the root cause of the phantom line, suspecting a misinterpretation of entity data. I\'ve been analyzing vertex flags and the structure of `LEADER` entities. While `LEADER` points have led to some conclusions, I\'m now exploring `LWPOLYLINE` representation within LibreDWG, examining `raw.points` and `raw.shape` to understand the data. I\'m focusing on why a plain line is rendered where an annotation should be!\n\n\n**Fixing Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the phantom line is due to erroneous polyline closure. It\'s caused by the incorrect handling of the `closed` flag in `libreDwgLoader.ts`, where `raw.shape` was incorrectly used. By also using `(rawFlag & 1)` and additional logic, I\'ve matched AutoCAD DXF behavior. The fix should resolve the diagonal lines!\n\n\n**Uncovering Shape Logic**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! I realized `raw.shape` always evaluates to `true` in JavaScript! This meant all polylines were incorrectly marked as closed, causing `ctx.closePath()` to draw spurious diagonal lines from the end to the start of open polylines. Specifically, the phantom line was due to open polylines being mistakenly closed! I\'m now understanding this is DXF related, and I\'ll need to focus on group 70 flags!\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'ve discovered `raw.shape` always evaluates as true, causing incorrect polyline closure and the appearance of phantom lines. Now, I\'m refactoring by using `rawFlag & 1` for closure and fixing the closure check in `modelExpander.ts`. This ensures correct interpretation of closure flags. This new closure logic should eliminate unwanted diagonal lines.\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the polyline closure logic in `modelExpander.ts`, particularly the conditions for preventing spurious diagonal closing lines. I\'m focusing on the distance check between the last and first vertices, as well as the delta-x and delta-y values. I\'ve noted a distance threshold and am now adding additional conditions to determine that these should never be connected! I\'m also examining the handling of internal jumps within polylines to ensure that disconnected segments are not erroneously connected.\n\n\n**Refining Polyline Disconnections**\n\nI\'m focusing now on preventing unintended line segments between disparate drawing elements within polylines. I\'m leveraging `isInterViewJump` to detect significant jumps in polylines, specifically those with a large delta-x, delta-y, and a length exceeding a threshold. This guarantees disconnected polylines. I\'ve noted that large dx and dy values are key to identifying disparate sections.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Detection**\n\nI\'m now implementing new artifact detection based on insights from image analysis and existing logic. I\'ve developed a rule to remove lines where both delta-x and delta-y exceed 600 units, the length is greater than 1000, and the length is over 12% of the minimum drawing span. This focuses on removing diagonal lines not on layers associated with legitimate drawing elements.\n\n\n**Preserving Orthogonal Lines**\n\nI\'ve just realized that deleting *orthogonal lines* is unacceptable because it would remove critical elements like the yellow outer border. I\'ve switched my focus to reviewing the extent calculation logic, specifically the percentile clamping in `modelExpander.ts`, to ensure these lines are preserved in the drawing.\n\n\n**Evaluating Extent Calculation**\n\nI\'m now investigating the extent calculation, and I\'ve found a critical issue in `modelExpander.ts`. The percentile clamping, specifically `low = 50` and `high = 9958`, eliminates the outer border vertices! This means the calculated drawing extents become the machine\'s bounding box instead of the full drawing sheet. Consequently, the camera zooms in on the machine, and the yellow outer border gets cut off. I need a solution!\n\n\n**Refining Extent Calculation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to correctly calculate drawing extents, ensuring the yellow border is included. I\'m considering professional CAD viewers and noticing that they handle the full drawing sheet. I\'m focusing on the significant coordinate gap between stray points at (0, 0) and the border. I will discard points close to the origin and keep points from the border. I\'m now testing to see if the yellow border lines were drawn at all.\n\n\n**Restoring Essential Borders**\n\nI\'m now focused on the missing yellow border, and confirmed it was being deleted by `isArtifactStreakSegment`. The border, orthogonal and exceeding a length threshold, was incorrectly deemed an artifact. I\'ve re-examined the criteria and will remove this removal to preserve the border, title block and grid ticks.\n\n\n**Investigating Dimension Retrieval**\n\nI\'m now investigating how dimension data is retrieved, and I\'m looking at the handling of dimension entities with block names in the DWG loader. I\'m focusing on the `raw` object in LibreDWG and checking if it populates the correct block related properties, like `raw.block`, `raw.blockName`, and `raw.name`, for dimension blocks. I want to see if this information is passed through to dimension entities.\n\n\n"
