  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Block Extraction**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on `cleanCadText(rawStr)`. I\'ve realized that directly stringifying the `raw.block` object results in \"[object Object]\". Therefore, I\'m working on a safe method to extract block names, specifically for `INSERT` and `DIMENSION` objects, by developing `extractBlockName(raw)`.\n\n\n**Refining Block Extraction Logic**\n\nI\'ve significantly refined the block extraction logic. I discovered that directly stringifying `raw.block` resulted in \"[object Object]\". Now, I\'m focusing on extracting block names safely, particularly for `INSERT` and `DIMENSION` objects, by developing a method to handle object references. Specifically, `val.name || val.blockName` extracts the block name, such as \"*D1\", from the BLOCK_RECORD object. Next, I am thinking about dimension objects, specifically the edge cases where dimension entities may not have a block and checking for cases like `raw.text` and `raw.user_text`.\n\n\n**Exploring Dimension Extraction**\n\nI\'m now investigating how to extract dimension data when the block name (`bname`) isn\'t found in the blocks. My focus is on extracting dimension text (`raw.text` or `raw.user_text`) and line data from definition points. I know AutoCAD typically uses anonymous blocks like `*D1`, and I need to account for color information in these blocks. Specifically, I am concerned with how color/ACI is handled within these dimension blocks, which commonly use `BYBLOCK` (ACI 0) or `BYLAYER` (ACI 256). I\'ll look at how `modelExpander.ts` handles those conditions, specifically looking at the effective layer and ACI logic.\n\n\n**Confirming Dimension Rendering**\n\nI\'ve confirmed how AutoCAD handles dimension entities, particularly anonymous blocks like `*D1`. When a `DIMENSION` entity is inserted, its layer (e.g., `DIM`) and ACI are considered, and these are then applied to the lines and text within the anonymous block. `e.layer === \'0\'` and `e.aci === 0` (BYBLOCK) conditions are key. This logic ensures accurate color representation matching the desktop rendering! Now, I will re-examine why dimensions were missing in the past.\n\n\n**Addressing Coordinate Anomalies**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a specific issue: all coordinates within the dimension block are being incorrectly shifted. Green lines and numbers are moved 42,659 units into the negative space. I\'ve pinpointed an issue with `extractBlockName` where object-based block references were being misinterpreted. Also, I\'ve noted that `isDim` was not consistently setting base points in `modelExpander.ts`. Finally, the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function in `modelExpander.ts` was deleting orthogonal lines, including the outer frame and extension lines.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Filtering**\n\nI\'m now refining the artifact filtering logic within `modelExpander.ts`. I\'ve realized that even if a polyline is marked as closed, large distances between the last and first points, coupled with significant changes in both the x and y directions, indicate it shouldn\'t be closed, so I will clear `e.closed = false`. Also, diagonal lines or segments exceeding specific length and delta thresholds represent artifacts that need to be filtered. Specifically, the artifacts include disparate viewport or machine cluster connecting lines. Finally, I\'ve identified that the canvas rendering in `CADCanvas.tsx` requires all vertex coordinates to be valid finite numbers to prevent disconnected line segments. I now need to consider the yellow outer border in `image_293c8b98.png`.\n\n\n**Restoring Title Block Visibility**\n\nI\'m now focused on the missing yellow rectangle frame in the title block. I realized this was due to `isArtifactStreakSegment` in `modelExpander.ts` deleting it. Removing the orthogonal line filter from this function should render the frame. I\'ve confirmed that the title block has magenta lines and text but, again, the yellow rectangle frame is missing. I will verify if any layers are hidden or invisible in `modelExpander.ts`, and I need to check `CADCanvas.tsx`.\n\n\n"
