  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Microscopic Entities**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how to handle \"zero-length lines,\" which I\'ve identified as microscopic degenerate points. The key is in that `Math.hypot` condition. It seems those lines with a tiny distance between their start and end points need special treatment. I need to figure out what this special treatment is and consider how it impacts the broader system.\n\n\n**Addressing Degenerate Cases**\n\nI\'ve been examining different entities and their edge cases. I\'m now exploring how to handle `LWPOLYLINE` objects. Duplicate consecutive vertices that produce zero-length segments are being filtered, along with cases where the vertices create a degenerate polyline or microscopic point. Infinite segments on the polyline also need to be identified. For circles, I\'m identifying microscopic dots and excessively large circles as degenerate cases as well.\n\n\n**Defining Edge Cases**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a range of entities and their boundary conditions. For circles, if I find infinite or invalid coordinates for center they will return null. Arcs with small radii or invalid coordinates, or zero-length, are also being addressed with null returns. I\'m also now handling degenerate ellipses and texts, and invalid inserts by checking their scales and positions.\n\n\n"
