  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Camera Inputs**\n\nI\'m currently focused on `CADCanvas.tsx`, specifically lines 1010-1020, to understand how various user inputs influence the camera. I\'m looking closely at how `camX`, `camY`, `zoom`, `rotation`, and other variables update in response to mouse or finger movements during interaction. My goal is to grasp how the camera\'s state changes.\n\n\n**Investigating Canvas Resets**\n\nI\'ve discovered an interesting detail about how the canvas behaves during panning. Changes to `camX` and `camY` in `App.tsx` trigger a re-render and a complete tear-down and re-bind of event listeners within `CADCanvas.tsx`. More significantly, setting `canvas.width` or `canvas.height` within the `ResizeObserver` effect, specifically lines 789-790, causes the browser to clear the entire canvas. This explains the visual reset I\'ve been observing during panning.\n\n\n**Examining Flickering Canvas**\n\nI\'m now diving into the flickering canvas issue, focusing on `CADCanvas.tsx`\'s render loop. Lines 403-420, in particular. It seems the background color is causing a complete re-draw on every render cycle which clears the canvas, rather than just redrawing the objects. The background is being filled on every frame, which may cause the re-draw of the canvas and explains the flickering. The render is being re-run, however, not during panning.\n\n\n**Understanding Render Timing**\n\nI\'m now investigating the order of events during canvas rendering. I see that user input triggers state updates in `App.tsx`, leading to a re-render of `CADCanvas`. This, in turn, causes a re-execution of the `useEffect` hook. I realize the canvas is cleared by `ctx.fillRect(0, 0, cw, ch)` *before* drawing complex shapes. It\'s likely that a browser paint in-between these operations could be problematic, especially with the synchronous render and large number of drawing operations for each frame. The sequence now appears to be: state update, re-render, `useEffect` execution, and then drawing.\n\n\n"
