  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Data Flow**\n\nI\'ve been examining the data flow within the application, and noticed every mouse movement triggers updates to both `mouseWorld` via `setMouseWorld` and `camX, camY` via `onUpdateCamera` in `src/App.tsx`. Further, I\'ve tracked the mousemove event within `CADCanvas.tsx` at lines 852-871, which appears to be where the data interaction occurs. I\'m focusing on that component now.\n\n\n**Analyzing Pan Efficiency**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the performance impact of panning. The `onMouseMove` function, found within `CADCanvas.tsx`, is called incredibly frequently during pan operations\342\200\224between 60 and 120 times per second! Each invocation updates both the `mouseWorld` state and the camera position (`camX`, `camY`). I think that the frequency of these updates could contribute to performance issues. I plan to test ways to reduce the amount of updates fired during pan.\n\n\n**Investigating Dependency Updates**\n\nI\'ve discovered the `mousemove` listener\'s effect in `CADCanvas.tsx` has dependencies on `camX`, `camY`, and zoom. Consequently, every mouse movement triggers a re-render of both `App.tsx` and `CADCanvas`. This effect cleans up and re-adds all event listeners, creating new closures for `onMouseMove` with the new camera state. I\'m now looking at the `dragStartCamRef.current` usage and how it is updated on `mousedown` and `touchstart`, specifically, where the `onMouseMove` closure is re-created. I think this might be a key factor.\n\n\n**Identifying Flicker Origins**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the potential causes of canvas flickering. The key issues seem to stem from events firing during React re-renders. Without `requestAnimationFrame`, synchronous updates trigger a clear/redraw cycle, leading to the blank/drawing sequence that causes the flicker. Specifically, constant clearing and re-drawing of the canvas in the `useEffect` render loop, at lines 403-418 in `CADCanvas.tsx`, is another major culprit. I think this is where I need to focus.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Render Issues**\n\nI\'ve homed in on the canvas rendering process. The `useEffect` hook in `CADCanvas.tsx` synchronously executes the `render()` function on the main thread, clearing the canvas and then attempting numerous drawing operations. Crucially, I see that the code declares `animId` but never calls `requestAnimationFrame`. This means every React render triggers an immediate clear and redraw, which could be an issue. If painting occurs at the wrong moment, the user sees a blank frame, or a partially drawn canvas. This explains the flickering.\n\n\n**Fixing the Flicker**\n\nI\'m now implementing `requestAnimationFrame` to alleviate flicker during rapid camera movements, addressing synchronous redraws within `useEffect`. I\'m replacing the synchronous calls to `render()` with an asynchronous approach using `requestAnimationFrame`, with cancellation of previous frames to prevent redundant calls. I\'m also now testing an offscreen canvas for double-buffering as a superior method to further eliminate flicker when painting many entities on the canvas. I\'m now testing it! I\'m also examining `ResizeObserver` for other potential contributions to the flickering, to determine if it is a third factor.\n\n\n"
