  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Mouse Interactions**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `onUpdateMouseWorld` function within `CADCanvas.tsx` and have found a key trigger \342\200\223 every mouse movement calls it. This updates `mouseWorld` in `App.tsx`, and that\'s causing App to re-render in React with every motion. I need to figure out why this is, and the implications of this.\n\n\n**Investigating Event Bindings**\n\nI\'ve discovered `CADCanvas.tsx` is unbinding and re-binding all mouse/touch event listeners whenever `camX`, `camY`, or `zoom` change. This happens because the `useEffect`\'s dependency array includes these state variables. This is a very inefficient method, especially during drag and pan interactions, which are almost constant.\n\n\n**Optimizing Event Handling**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down on the root cause and realized the inefficiency stems from `useEffect`\'s re-mounting of event listeners on every frame, due to changes in `camX`, `camY`, `zoom`, and `rotation`. This can cause events to drop and create jerky animations. I\'m focusing on why those are dependencies, and it looks like a `useRef` can store these values as a mutable object, updating within a separate effect. That would permit attaching/detaching the listeners only on mount/unmount and avoid the churn. I\'m also examining the render loop now.\n\n\n**Tracing Render Operations**\n\nI\'m now tracing the execution of the `render` function within `CADCanvas.tsx` to get a clearer picture of what the render loop does. I\'ve pinpointed `ctx.fillRect(0, 0, cw, ch)`, within the `useEffect` hook, which clears the canvas with every frame. This clearing, combined with drawing operations, is causing latency, especially on devices or with many lines. The `alpha: false` option is important here as it impacts how the canvas composites in the browser.\n\n\n**Eliminating Flicker Concerns**\n\nI\'m now laser-focused on the flicker issue. I\'ve realized that the compositor paints a blank canvas if it runs in the middle of executing canvas commands! Specifically, the issue is that it runs after `ctx.fillRect(0, 0, cw, ch)` but before `ctx.stroke()` finishes. This causes a rapid, violent flicker at 60fps! The solution is to use double-buffering using an offscreen canvas: drawing the background, the grid, and batches to the in-memory buffer, not the visible canvas.\n\n\n**Implementing Double Buffering**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implementation details of the double-buffering solution. I\'m focusing on drawing all elements like polylines, circles, arcs, and text to the offscreen canvas (`offCtx`). Once this offscreen frame is fully rendered, I will copy the completed image to the visible canvas in a single, instantaneous `ctx.drawImage` call. This ensures that the visible canvas is never blank, eliminating any flicker for the user.\n\n\n**Refining Input Throttling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the `onUpdateMouseWorld` function. I\'m going to throttle its execution. During panning interactions, which occur frequently, the user isn\'t actively selecting or measuring. It\'s clear that calling `onUpdateMouseWorld` at such a high frequency (e.g. 120 times per second) is redundant, and this triggers excessive re-renders. My solution is to only call this function when the user is not dragging the screen, or implementing throttling to limit the re-renders. I\'ve also identified a solution to reduce re-rendering based on the camera and mode through the use of refs.\n\nI\'m now investigating other sources of possible flicker. Specifically, I\'m checking the `CADCanvas.tsx` file for possible causes. I\'m focusing on line 133, which sets up the `canvasDimensions` state variable. I am checking if this, too, causes unnecessary re-renders. The `canvasDimensions` is in the dependency array of the render effect which I think this might be causing flickering.\n\n\n"
