    thinking: "**Tracing the Offscreen Canvas**\n\nI\'m now tracing the execution flow, specifically the offscreen canvas. I\'ve begun to examine how the `render` function utilizes an offscreen canvas to optimize drawing. I am observing the implementation and the potential issues that may arise with the reference of `offscreenRef`. I\'m starting to visualize how the canvas element is created and initialized within the context of the render lifecycle.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Rendering Process**\n\nI\'ve been scrutinizing the `offCtx` usage extensively. I\'ve noted every element \342\200\223 background, grid, previews, renderBatches, geometric entities, text, highlights, measurements \342\200\223 is drawn to `offCtx`. This culminates in a single `ctx.drawImage` call to the visible canvas. This reveals that the visible canvas is never cleared mid-frame! Further, I\'ve observed how the use of `requestAnimationFrame` helps coalesce multiple camera updates within the same frame, thereby optimizing the process.\n\n\n**Observing the Frame Updates**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the drawing happens precisely once per VSYNC frame, eliminating any flicker during dragging or panning at 60/120 FPS. This confirms the smoothness. I am now examining how `onUpdateMouseWorld` is called within the `onMouseMove` event handler, specifically how it calculates world coordinates from screen coordinates, accounting for zoom, rotation, and panning. I\'m focusing on how camera updates are handled within this process.\n\n\n**Optimizing Dragging Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the dragging interaction, particularly when panning. I\'ve realized that calling `onUpdateMouseWorld` on every pixel during dragging is unnecessary and causes performance issues, due to the top-level re-renders. My current thought process is to only update `onUpdateMouseWorld` when hovering or at the end of the drag. I\'m also examining the role of `onUpdateCamera` during pan and considering the potential of utilizing `requestAnimationFrame` for it, considering that in React 19, the state setters are batched.\n\n\n"
