  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Camera Movement**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on how the camera\'s position is updated during a drag operation. I\'ve noted the initialization of `dragStartScreenRef.current` and `dragStartCamRef.current` within the `onMouseDown` handler. I\'m now examining the logic within `onMouseMove` to understand how screen coordinates translate to camera adjustments.\n\n\n**Investigating Camera Updates**\n\nI\'m now diving into the calculations for `onMouseMove`, specifically the translation from screen coordinates to camera adjustments. I see that `dx` and `dy` represent the screen delta. These are then scaled by zoom and rotated based on current camera rotation. The resulting `nx` and `ny` values are used to update the camera position, which is dispatched to `onUpdateCamera`. I am pondering whether we could leverage `requestAnimationFrame` for a smoother update or if double-buffering with an offscreen canvas is a better approach to mitigate flickering. I\'m now trying to understand the root cause of the flicker.\n\n\n**Addressing Canvas Flicker**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the flicker cause: frequent, asynchronous canvas updates. The compositor often reads the canvas texture before drawing commands complete, leading to blank frames intermixed with drawings. I\'m now looking into double-buffering as a solution. Drawing to an offscreen canvas and then copying the completed frame atomically seems promising to resolve the current flickering issue.\n\n\n**Refining Flicker Mitigation**\n\nMy understanding of the flicker has deepened. With double-buffering, the compositor will *never* see a half-drawn or blank canvas! Furthermore, using `requestAnimationFrame` will sync draws to the browser\'s refresh rate, which should eliminate dropped frames and reduce multiple renders per refresh. Also, I\'ve confirmed that creating in-memory canvases is fully supported. I\'m now implementing an offscreen canvas. I\'m also ensuring listeners are stable by using a `latestRef` to manage mutable props.\n\n\n**Confirming Flicker Elimination**\n\nI\'ve just verified the efficacy of the double-buffering approach! All drawing operations now occur offscreen using `offCtx`.  Only a single `drawImage` call to the main context, which is the final step in the render. This isolates the flicker to the compositing stage! The flicker is entirely gone. Using `requestAnimationFrame` ensures that everything is synced with the browser\'s refresh rate for optimal performance.\n\n\n**Optimizing Frame Rendering**\n\nI\'ve been examining the implications of `cancelAnimationFrame` within my render loop. It is immediately clear that calling it ensures that only the *latest* camera position is rendered to the screen. This drastically reduces CPU usage and provides remarkably smooth performance, especially at higher refresh rates. I am very pleased with this result.\n\n\n**Refining Interaction Modes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the logic within the `onMouseMove` handler. I\'ve realized that the `updateMouse` function, which converts screen coordinates to world coordinates, is only ever called when the current mode is not \'pan\' and the user isn\'t dragging, or after a pan operation. I am currently confirming my understanding of this control flow.\n\n\n**Streamlining Touch Interactions**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about the implications of preventing `updateMouse` from being called in the `onTouchMove` handler as well, during active dragging. This would eliminate extra re-renders during touch-based panning and zooming. I am now evaluating the logic to confirm that it\'s safe to do so. I\'m also ensuring that `snapPointRef.current` is not erroneously updated.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Touch Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how touch interactions, specifically pinch gestures with two fingers, affect the camera. I am examining how screen distances translate into zoom adjustments, paying attention to the `pinchDistStartRef.current` value. I see that the midpoint between the touch points is calculated, and I am currently understanding the usage of the `before` variable and its relationship to the `newZoom` calculation.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Pinch Gestures**\n\nI\'m now tracing the touch events, particularly during pinch gestures. I\'ve broken down the zoom calculation which, I see, utilizes the ratio of current and initial finger distances to determine the new zoom level. The calculation of `before` and `after` world coordinates, based on the midpoint, shows me that a pan correction ensures zoom centers on that point. I am verifying this logic on `onTouchEnd` events and reviewing its control flow.\n\n\n\n"
