  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Mouse Interaction**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the `onMouseDown` event listener (lines 902-1049). I\'ve noted it calculates the canvas\'s bounding rectangle and extracts client X/Y coordinates from the mouse event. Next I will explore how these coordinates are transformed and used within the larger context, as well as review the listener for `onTouchStart` in order to compare the approach.\n\n\n**Examining Mouse Movement**\n\nI\'m now deep diving into the `onMouseMove` handler. I\'ve noted it retrieves the canvas\'s bounding rectangle and computes screen coordinates. These are used in conjunction with camera parameters like zoom, rotation, and current mode to determine changes in the camera position. I\'m focusing on how the world coordinates are updated to reflect the panning action.\n\n\n**Refining Drag and Pan**\n\nI\'ve just refined the panning logic within `onMouseMove`. I\'ve realized that conditionally skipping `onUpdateMouseWorld` during drag significantly boosts performance. I\'m seeing that by skipping this, I avoid unnecessary re-renders in `App.tsx` on every tiny mouse movement, making the panning much smoother. Now, the `onMouseUp` event accurately updates mouse coordinates, ensuring a good response.\n\n\n**Investigating Zoom Implementation**\n\nI\'m now examining the `onWheel` event handler. I see that it uses `WheelEvent` data to calculate a zoom factor, updating the camera position based on the mouse\'s position relative to the canvas. The implementation also clamps the `newZoom` value to a reasonable range. I\'m noting the use of `screenToWorld` to translate screen coordinates.\n\n\n**Exploring Touch Input**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the `onTouchStart` event. I see that single-finger touches initiate dragging, storing starting coordinates and camera position. Two-finger touches, on the other hand, disable dragging and are used for pinch-to-zoom, measuring initial distance. I\'m focusing on the differences in handling of single versus multi-touch events, and how these states are managed.\n\n\n**Evaluating Touch Movement**\n\nI\'m now deep into `onTouchMove`. I see that it cancels default behavior and calculates the canvas bounding rectangle. With single touches and dragging active, I see the mode determines what happens. In \"measure\" or \"calibrate\", I can update snap points and a touch loupe. Otherwise, the coordinates are used to calculate camera movement, taking zoom, rotation, and current camera position into account.\n\n\n**Dissecting Pinch Zoom**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the pinch-to-zoom logic within `onTouchMove`. I am seeing that, with two fingers, the code calculates the distance between the touch points. It then utilizes this distance to compute the zoom level, adjusting the camera position accordingly, and storing the current distance. Finally, I noticed `onTouchEnd` has also been called, and the touch loupe is hidden.\n\n\n"
