  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Canvas Interactions**\n\nI\'ve been working on defining the `CADCanvasHandle` interface. I\'ve included `captureThumbnail`, `fitExtents`, and `getCanvas` methods. I\'ve also just added `requestRender` to explicitly allow for on-demand rendering. I\'m hoping this interface provides a robust way to interact with the CAD canvas component.\n\n\n**Analyzing Render Requests**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of \"request-on-demand\" rendering. The goal is to avoid continuous rendering loops in favor of explicitly triggered updates, either by calling `requestRender()` or in response to visual changes. I\'ve been detailing the mechanism: specifically coalescing render requests and performing a diff against a snapshot of the visual state to avoid redundant renders. This includes details on the snapshot parameters.\n\n\n**Optimizing On-Demand Rendering**\n\nI\'m focusing on ensuring zero CPU/GPU usage when idle, and preventing redundant renders caused by parent re-renders. My approach involves a `requestRender()` mechanism. It should ensure responsiveness to user interaction. I\'ve been structuring the `CADCanvas.tsx` component with `offscreenRef`, `animFrameRef`, and `isDirtyRef` to control the rendering. The logic needs to efficiently capture visual state changes. The goal is 60fps responsiveness upon demand.\n\n\n**Defining Render State**\n\nI\'m now detailing the snapshot state for render comparison. It needs to efficiently detect visual changes. I\'ve defined a `RenderStateSnapshot` interface with properties for width, height, camera position, zoom, rotation, theme, grid visibility, and object snap. Crucially, I\'m working to make signatures for complex data like layers, measure points, and selected entities. I\'m looking at quick hash algorithms to ensure reliability, and fast computation.\n\n\n**Refining Render Function**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `render` function and how it\'s defined and called. I realize that placing `render` inside `useEffect` creates a new closure with each re-render. I\'m exploring solutions like using `useCallback` or making `render` a stable function that references the latest state through refs or props. I\'m currently assessing the implications of each approach to ensure efficient rendering and state management. I\'ve also reconsidered the usage of the `requestRender` call: I need a `force` option to be able to manually force an updated render!\n\n\n"
