  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Animation Control**\n\nOkay, I\'m examining the animation frame management within `CADCanvas.tsx`, specifically lines 790-805. It looks like I\'m canceling any existing animation frame before attempting to start a new one, which is expected. I am checking if there is an animation frame to cancel, and then canceling it if it exists.\n\n\n**Implementing Demand Rendering**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a \"request-on-demand\" rendering approach. I\'ve defined the key aspects: an explicit `requestRender()` mechanism. The new approach will include change detection and frame state caching using `lastRenderStateRef`, and marks `isDirtyRef.current = true`. This will manage the redraw scheduling on the animation frame.\n\n\n**Evaluating State Comparison**\n\nI\'m now diving into the state comparison logic within the `render()` function. My aim is to efficiently determine if a redraw is necessary before executing any potentially expensive canvas operations. The plan is to compare critical visual state components, like canvas dimensions, camera parameters, and entity data, against the `lastRenderStateRef`. If everything matches and `isDirtyRef.current` is false, I intend to skip the redraw entirely.\n\n\n**Finalizing Change Detection**\n\nI\'ve just finished implementing the final piece: updating the render state and resetting the dirty flag. I\'m now setting `lastRenderStateRef.current` to the new snapshot and `isDirtyRef.current = false`. This will complete the loop of change detection. Furthermore, I\'ve integrated triggers for change detection within `useEffect` hooks, marking `isDirtyRef.current = true` and calling `requestRender()` whenever props like camera parameters, entity data, or canvas dimensions change. Finally, I\'ve integrated on-demand rendering triggering from user interactions via mouse movement within the `measure` mode, to ensure responsive updates.\n\n\n**Integrating Snap Logic**\n\nI\'ve just added snap point change detection. Now, when the snap point changes (e.g., snapping or unsnapping), I mark the canvas as dirty and call `requestRender()`. This solves the redraw issue that was occurring when `snapPointRef` changed, because now the canvas will update immediately and cleanly. This completes the cycle of making on-demand rendering work for all changes.\n\n\n\n**Refactoring Rendering Dependency**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential optimization for the `render` function by checking dependencies. I am curious if the function can read either the latest props or the `latestRef.current`. I\'m verifying what elements are contained within `latestRef.current`. This refactoring should help minimize unnecessary re-renders.\n\n\n**Analyzing Render Stability**\n\nI\'m now focusing on stabilizing the render function. I\'ve re-examined lines 405-433, specifically the `latestRef` structure and what elements are stored within it. I am verifying if the render function can depend on the `latestRef.current` values rather than constantly changing props, which would make the function stable and minimize re-renders. I am now evaluating the implications and benefits of the render function\'s stability.\n\n\n**Evaluating Render Triggers**\n\nI\'m now investigating when `requestRender()` is triggered and the data it accesses. Specifically, I\'m examining the `lastRenderStateRef` to see how it stores state. Then, I\'m analyzing the data passed to the render function to better evaluate the render function\'s inputs. I believe this will help identify potential render bottlenecks.\n\n\n\n**Validating Render Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the render dependencies. I am checking if I can use the same state keys to determine render-function stability. I am evaluating whether the comparison should include `snapKey`, `layersKey` and `selectedId` to determine the validity of the render function state.\n\n\n**Tracing Render Cases**\n\nI\'m now tracing all potential rendering scenarios. I\'m focusing on scenarios where the user isn\'t interacting, leading to zero rendered frames. The state comparison logic verifies if a redraw is needed, and if the state hasn\'t changed, rendering is skipped. I\'m confirming this behavior ensures performance optimization during periods of inactivity.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Rendering Triggers**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the rendering triggers. I\'ve pinpointed a common scenario: when the `CADCanvas` re-renders due to unrelated state changes (like a top bar update), the `useEffect` hook still ensures efficient operation. Even if `requestRender()` is triggered, the `render()` function performs a quick state comparison and avoids drawing if the visual dependencies haven\'t changed. If visual dependencies *have* changed, like camera coordinates due to a user pan, `render()` will draw the new frame.\n\n\n**Refining Frame Scheduling**\n\nI\'m now refining frame scheduling. I\'ve realized multiple touch/mouse events trigger `isDirtyRef.current = true` within the same interval, ensuring only one draw per refresh. This is efficient. In measure/calibrate modes, snap point changes (`newSnap`) now trigger an immediate redraw using the OSNAP green marker box. If the snap remains null, no redraw occurs, which will provide improved performance. Canvas/window resize updates also correctly trigger redraws as intended.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Thumbnail Capture**\n\nI\'m now investigating thumbnail capture and its relationship with the render cycle. If `captureThumbnail` is called before the canvas has rendered after loading, a synchronous `render()` call is triggered via `requestRender(true)`. I\'m evaluating the use of the imperative handle to get the canvas and capture the thumbnail immediately, or read from `canvasRef.current`. I\'m also confirming the availability of `worldToScreen` and `screenToWorld` transforms.\n\n\n**Verifying Transform Functions**\n\nI\'m now deep in the details of `worldToScreen` and `screenToWorld` transforms. I\'ve extracted `ToScreen` and `screenToWorld` as `useCallback` functions. I\'m verifying the math for these transformations. I\'m ensuring they correctly convert between world and screen coordinates, taking zoom, rotation, and camera position into account.\n\n\n**Confirming Transform Inclusion**\n\nI\'m verifying that `worldToScreen` is included in `latestRef.current`, alongside `screenToWorld`, to support coordinate transformations within my rendering loop. This will streamline the data handling for mouse interactions. I am further reviewing how these transforms are used within `onMouseMove` in measure/calibrate modes, specifically lines 928-933.\n\n\n**Optimizing Snap Interactions**\n\nI\'m now confirming the efficiency of my snap logic in measure and calibrate modes, both for mouse and touch interactions. Only changes to the snap target trigger a redraw. When the mouse or a touch moves over empty space, there are no redraws. I\'ve re-examined the code, confirming that only the snap point changing initiates a redraw.\n\n\n**Validating Input Parameters**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the inputs to `render()` and when `requestRender()` is called. I\'m confirming `latestRef.current` includes `worldToScreen` and all related properties that are necessary for accurate renderings. I need to be sure the canvas is only redrawn if any visual parameters actually change. I am re-examining the rendering loop to ensure this is always the case.\n\n\n**Validating Render Parameters**\n\nI\'m now deeply engaged in confirming the integrity of the input parameters passed into my `render()` function and the triggers for `requestRender()`. I am examining `latestRef.current` to ensure the presence of all required data, including `worldToScreen`, to guarantee accurate and optimized renderings. My focus remains fixed on ensuring canvas redraws only occur when visual parameters genuinely change, re-evaluating the rendering loop to ensure this is always upheld.\n\n\n**Confirming Render Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now focused on the specific visual parameters used in my state comparison, to ensure render stability. The code confirms canvas redraws only occur with visual parameter changes, which is working. I am re-examining the rendering loop to ensure visual dependencies drive the render calls. I am evaluating how to optimize by reducing or removing dependencies, if possible.\n\n\n**Analyzing Double-Buffering**\n\nI\'m now diving into the double-buffering implementation. I\'m verifying the atomic copy from the offscreen canvas to the visible one is flicker-free. I\'m checking if the width and height of the offscreen canvas are updated. Additionally, I am confirming the render calls are appropriately scheduled.\n\n\n\n**Investigating Canvas Resizing**\n\nI\'m now investigating the `handleResize()` function and its interaction with canvas dimensions and rendering. I\'m verifying how changes to canvas width and height affect the rendering process. I\'m focusing on lines 830-845 to confirm resize events appropriately trigger redraws with the new dimensions.\n\n\n**Integrating Request Function**\n\nI\'m now integrating `requestRender()` into `latestRef`. This will let mouse and touch listeners trigger renders directly, improving responsiveness. I am ensuring imperative handle and `latestRef` correctly interact to allow for on-demand renders.\n\n\n"
