    thinking: "**Locating Specific Functions**\n\nOkay, I\'ve zeroed in on the code locations: `updateTouchLoupe` is at line 327 and `handlePointTap` is at 368. I also spotted the `offscreenRef` definition at line 402. Now, I\'m considering how best to integrate `useImperativeHandle`. It could stay at line 313, or I could move it to call `latestRef.current.requestRender()` or just `requestRender()` if I place it differently.\n\n\n**Refining Component Placement**\n\nI\'m now completely happy with the placement of `useImperativeHandle` after `requestRender`, it is clean. Lines 402 to 826 in `CADCanvas.tsx` are my focus: I have reviewed them and can see that the critical refs (`offscreenRef`, `animFrameRef`, `latestRef`) are defined, followed by the `render` function within `useEffect` and finally, a `requestAnimationFrame` call also in `useEffect`.\n\n\n**Analyzing useEffect Dependencies**\n\nI\'m deep in the `useEffect` dependency analysis, particularly lines 806-826. I\'ve begun restructuring, starting with defining the `RenderStateSnapshot` interface to streamline data handling. I\'ve also identified and defined the relevant refs: `offscreenRef`, `animFrameRef`, `isDirtyRef`, and `lastRenderStateRef`. This groundwork will allow for cleaner, more predictable dependency management.\n\n\n**Defining Render Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the `render` structure, specifically the canvas-related aspects within `CADCanvas.tsx`. My current focus is lines 480-502. I\'m carefully reviewing the callback function within `useCallback` to understand how the canvas dimensions and rendering process are handled. The aim is to integrate the request-on-demand render more effectively with the imperative handle I\'m implementing.\n\n\n**Calculating Render Keys**\n\nI am currently working on calculating quick visual signatures for dirty checking to optimize the render performance. I\'m focusing on lines 600-650 in `CADCanvas.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m generating `snapKey`, `layersKey`, and `selectedId` to represent render state changes efficiently. I\'m considering using these keys to determine if a re-render is necessary before actually executing the render function.\n\n\n**Optimizing Dirty Checks**\n\nI\'ve just focused on lines 710-745 in `CADCanvas.tsx` to optimize dirty checking logic, implementing an early exit for unchanged states. I am confirming a clean render bypasses redraw entirely if there are zero visual changes. I\'m verifying the inclusion of a state snapshot recording to manage dirty state updates.\n\n\n**Confirming Render Logic**\n\nI\'ve just dived into lines 750-800 to refine the final rendering process. I have completed integrating the double-buffering logic. The current focus is ensuring efficient offscreen canvas updates and a fast atomic copy to the visible canvas, using the calculated render keys to determine if a full redraw is necessary. I\'ve confirmed double buffering and dirty state management.\n\n\n**Implementing Double Buffering**\n\nI\'ve just added flicker-free updates by implementing double buffering via `drawImage`. I\'m using `offscreen` and `onScreenCtx` to manage canvas updates. I also defined `requestRender` to coalesce renders, which is now triggered by changes in reactive props via `useEffect`. The main focus is the double buffering combined with `requestAnimationFrame` for a smooth, flicker-free on-screen rendering.\n\n\n**Refining Render Triggers**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the render triggers, especially within `handleResize`, `onMouseMove`, and `onTouchMove`. I\'ve added a check for the canvas dimensions to trigger a render. I\'m also now carefully reviewing how `snapPointRef` changes affect the render state, ensuring that `requestRender` is called only when necessary.\n\n\n"
