  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Minimap Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the minimap\'s rendering behavior. It seems the minimap only displays when the `isOpen` state is true. I\'ve checked that in the provided images (Image 1 and Image 2), the minimap is closed (`isOpen: false`). I\'m looking at `CADCanvas.tsx` to understand what\'s happening during panning, specifically when `isDraggingRef.current && mode === \'pan\'`.\n\n\n**Tracing Camera Updates**\n\nI\'ve traced the camera updates during panning. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed that mouse and touch move events trigger `onUpdateCamera`. This function subsequently updates camera state (`camX`, `camY`, `zoom`) in `App.tsx`, causing a re-render. This re-render then propagates the updated camera parameters to the `CADCanvas` component. Within `CADCanvas`, `useEffect` is activated, which sets the `isDirtyRef` flag and calls `requestRender()`. I need to figure out how this is connected to what I\'m looking at in `Minimap.tsx`.\n\n\n**Examining Render Loop**\n\nI\'m now examining the render loop within `CADCanvas.tsx`, specifically how camera updates affect rendering. The `useEffect` hook triggers on changes to `camX`, `camY`, and other camera parameters. I\'m focusing on how `requestRender()` is called and how the `animFrameRef` is utilized to control animation frames. I\'m also looking at the cleanup function which cancels existing animation frames.\n\n\n**Investigating Frame Cancellation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the implications of canceling animation frames within `CADCanvas.tsx`. My current thought is centered on how frequent touch or mouse move events might lead to excessive frame cancellations. The current implementation, with `cancelAnimationFrame` within the cleanup function, could be causing significant stuttering and frame drops due to rapid re-renders. This is especially problematic with high-frequency touch events, where each event cancels the previous frame before it can even be painted. This has made me start to question: What is the canvas element?\n\n\n"
