    thinking: "**Analyzing Potential Sources**\n\nI\'m currently focused on identifying the origins of specific lines, particularly the long diagonal white ones, starting near the top-left machine in the provided image. I\'m leveraging the LibreDWG filter of `VIEWPORT` and revisiting the image for clues. The goal is to determine what these lines could represent and their relation to the overall context.\n\n\n**Pinpointing a Coordinate**\n\nI\'ve determined that multiple lines converge towards a specific coordinate or cluster. This point piques my interest. I\'m now contemplating what might exist at that intersection in an AutoCAD drawing. My initial hypothesis centers around the possibility of it being `(0, 0)`, or perhaps a block insertion origin. The coordinates from Photo 2 provide a point of reference; I\'m trying to deduce the location of the actual `(0, 0)` within the overall drawing context, particularly relative to the machines shown. I now see the lines trend towards the right, and I am reconsidering the machine on the right is a duplicate assembly view of the left machine.\n\n\n**Considering Block Inclusions**\n\nI\'m now contemplating the possibility that either the left or right machine in the image represents an insertion of a block within an AutoCAD drawing. The presence of these seemingly duplicate lines has led me down a train of thought: what if one is a duplicate insert? Could one be an insertion, and the other the base block? I\'m examining scenarios where the block\'s entities might be in different coordinate systems, or the insert\'s base point deviates from the block\'s default. I\'m revisiting the `modelExpander.ts` file, specifically lines 146-170, for clues on how blocks, dimensions, insertions, and coordinates are handled.\n\n\n**Interpreting Zoom-Based Artifacts**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical clue: the lines only appear at higher zoom levels, specifically in Photo 2. This suggests they are not inherently part of the original model data, or perhaps are handled differently at different zoom levels. I\'m considering that the long lines might be a rendering artifact or a visualization issue specific to how the model is displayed at different scales. This is a very interesting concept, and I plan to explore this much more deeply, as I revisit Photo 1 and Photo 2.\n\n\n**Investigating Zoom-Dependent Render**\n\nI\'m now focusing on *why* these lines only appear at specific zoom levels. I\'m investigating `CADCanvas.tsx`, specifically lines 698-747, to understand how zoom affects line rendering. My current thought is: was there a conditional rendering check based on zoom? I\'m examining the `batch.lineCoords` and `polylines` to see if zoom level plays a role in their visibility, perhaps a clipping issue. I\'m looking at how the coordinate calculations are performed within the canvas.\n\n\n**Exploring Polyline Closure**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in the implications of `polys[pIdx].closed`. This \'closed\' state of polylines is crucial. I see a connection: if a polyline is \'closed\', then the drawing method connects the last point back to the first. What if a polyline incorporates vertex data from more than one object? Could this be the cause of the extra line? I\'m re-examining the potential for a single polyline entity to span multiple objects, and whether this closure logic could create unexpected visuals.\n\n\n**Unraveling Cross-Machine Lines**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical insight into the origin of those diagonal lines. I\'m focusing on the possibility of a `batch.lines` entry where one end of the line is on the left machine, and the other end resides on the right machine. This is very interesting! I\'m now exploring how such an entity could be generated, and why. I\'m closely examining the `modelExpander.ts` code, especially where `batch.lines` are populated. I see something interesting in `isArtifactStreakSegment`.\n\n\n**Refining Diagonal Line Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the diagonal line generation logic, specifically the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function, lines 345-353. The code checks for `dx > 350 && dy > 350`, then applies additional checks. I\'m focusing on the shallow slope and its potential to generate false positives. I\'m recalling the drawing\'s dimensions. I now think that the conditional could be modified.\n\n\n**Questioning Sheet Integration**\n\nI\'m now deeply considering multiple sheets within the DWG. I\'ve realized the file contains two complete sheet borders side-by-side! My primary thought is, why are there unexpected lines connecting Sheet 1 to Sheet 2? My research shows AutoCAD does not render these lines, so the issue seems localized to the parser, or LibreDWG. I\'m focused on discovering the origin of these lines.\n\n\n"
