  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Vertex Flags**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of vertex flags within DWG/DXF files. I\'ve pinpointed an intriguing line (434) that sparked my investigation. Currently, I\'m trying to decipher what each bit represents within the vertex flags. I\'ve identified that bit 1 indicates a curve-fitted vertex, and bit 2 relates to the curve-fit tangent.\n\n\n**Analyzing Polyline Vertices**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the complexities of polyline vertices, specifically in the context of 2D vs. 3D variations and spline frame control points. I\'m also suspecting that LibreDWG might be introducing terminating vertices at the origin (0, 0) in the vlist, which creates an uninitialized dummy vertex, or terminating vertex. I\'m also now suspecting that this terminating vertex with an x and y of 0, 0 can cause problems when lines exist at the end.\n\n\n**Identifying Artifacts: Lines**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on these strange lines. They\'re clearly artifacts, crossing the machine\'s border in a way that just wouldn\'t happen in a CAD drawing. It\'s so strange, it\'s never done! These artifact lines break several drafting conventions, especially crossing borders. Now, my focus shifts to understanding *why* they exist and, critically, how to identify and filter them. I\'m checking the length, angle, and connectivity and comparing them with the existing `isArtifactStreakSegment` function.\n\n\n"
