  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Diagonal Movement**\n\nI\'ve been examining the code, specifically lines containing calculations involving `dx` and `dy`. I think the logic around the `isDiagonal` variable warrants closer examination. The conditions for what defines a diagonal movement seem potentially too strict, and I\'m currently wondering if those thresholds are the most optimal. I\'m focusing on the interaction between `len` and `maxDiag` as it relates to this.\n\n\n**Investigating Line Filtering**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical flaw in the filtering logic related to diagonal lines. Specifically, the thresholds set around `dy` (less than 350) and `len` (length) are not effectively filtering lines that are nearly horizontal or vertical, but very long. The `isDiagonal` check seems too strict, leading to a bypass when the change in `y` is small. Also, the condition `(len > 1000 || len > maxDiag * 0.15)` simplifies down to `len > 1000` because JavaScript short-circuits.\n\n\n**Defining Axis-Aligned Lines**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining what constitutes a legitimate long line within a CAD drawing context. Based on my observations, the key is axis alignment. Horizontal and vertical lines, like sheet borders and centerlines, are the most common valid long lines. Dimension witness lines are always short. I\'ve concluded that lines longer than 1500 units that aren\'t axis-aligned are highly suspect and should be filtered, particularly if they connect different subassemblies or views. I\'m going to update the filtering conditions to reflect this conclusion.\n\n\n**Examining Spline Handling**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the code handles SPLINE entities, specifically referencing `libreDwgLoader.ts` lines 608-620. My focus has shifted to the potential source of the suspect long lines, and I strongly suspect they originate from SPLINE definitions representing flexible tubes and wiring within the DWG file. I\'m scrutinizing how LibreDWG converts SPLINE data into LWPOLYLINE, and the implications of this conversion.\n\n\n**Unraveling Spline Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the cause of the long lines, which I suspect are linked to SPLINE entity processing. I\'ve been scrutinizing the `fitPoints` and `controlPoints` within LibreDWG\'s handling of these splines. The presence of both data types raises a red flag, especially the conversion from `SPLINE` to `LWPOLYLINE`. I\'m particularly interested in how tangent vectors might be misinterpreted as coordinates, potentially leading to these problematic long line artifacts. The AutoCAD DWG specification confirms this, stating that DXF group codes 12/13 define `startTangent` and `endTangent`. I\'m also suspecting that LibreDWG may treat tangent vectors as points if they are large, causing long rays to be drawn.\n\n\n"
