    thinking: "**Examining Specific Code**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `src/lib/dxfParser.ts` file, specifically lines 267-276. I\'m trying to understand the handling of SPLINE, SOLID, and TRACE entities, particularly how the vertex data is parsed. The conditional check for group codes 10-13 seems critical for extracting the x and y coordinates. I\'m focusing on the `parseFloat(v)` call and its impact on the data extraction.\n\n\n**Clarifying Spline Data**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery regarding how `dxfParser.ts` handles SPLINE entity data. It incorrectly groups control points, fit points, and tangent vectors together as vertices. The code incorrectly pushes the X, and Y values, from various group codes into `ent.vertices`, resulting in the tangents being treated as points, not vectors. This leads to unwanted line segments connecting these unrelated geometric elements. I need to fix this soon.\n\n\n**Investigating Diagonal Artifacts**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the root cause of the diagonal lines. Specifically, I am cross-referencing the structure of SPLINE entities in LibreDWG\342\200\224`control_points`, `fit_points`, `start_tangent`, and `end_tangent`\342\200\224with the incorrect vertex handling in `dxfParser.ts`. I also considered that the issue could be related to `isArtifactStreakSegment` in `modelExpander.ts`. I\'m starting to review how these tangent and fit points are handled in the loading and sanitization process.\n\n\n**Analyzing Polyline Logic**\n\nI\'m now investigating the polyline creation, particularly how \"streaks\" are handled, and how that relates to the jump check. I\'m focusing on lines 420-432, wondering if the streak segment could itself be a LINE entity. The sanitization process in `cadSanitizer.ts` only seems to filter based on length, and I now need to consider how `isArtifactStreakSegment` in `modelExpander.ts` is triggered.\n\n\n**Refining Artifact Detection**\n\nI\'ve been reevaluating the artifact detection logic in `modelExpander.ts`, particularly the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function. I now understand that a \"diagonal\" is defined by large `dx` and `dy` values, and this can be misleading. I\'m thinking about the case where `dy` might be small, but `dx` is enormous. It\'s a key observation that a streak crossing empty space between viewports is the common anomaly. Now, I will update the logic to look at the relation of the distance of the line and the max size of the machine. I\'m relating this back to how SPLINE data is being parsed, and how this relates to diagonal artifacts.\n\n\n**Isolating Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the DXF specification\'s structure for SPLINE entities. I\'m focused on the distinct group codes for control points (10, 20, 30), fit points (11, 21, 31), and tangent vectors (12/13, 22/23, 32/33). I realize that the previous parsing logic was incorrectly combining these different types of data, particularly when the tangent vectors were mistakenly treated as vertices. My code had pushed tangent vectors into `ent.vertices`, resulting in the unwanted diagonal lines connecting distant points. The machine coordinates exacerbated this. Now, I will implement a separation of the point types.\n\n\n**Refining Coordinate Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the handling of SPLINE entity points in `src/lib/dxfParser.ts`, specifically lines 608-619. I\'m focusing on the condition where `raw.fitPoints` or `raw.controlPoints` contain unexpected data, such as a redundant origin point or tangent vectors, which can lead to invalid segments. I\'m testing a new check for this. If a point is `(0, 0)` and all others are far away, or there\'s a huge jump between the last two points, I\'ll strip the leading or trailing point. Finally, I\'ll integrate this improvement into `isArtifactStreakSegment` in `modelExpander.ts`.\n\n\n**Adjusting Distance Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the `isArtifactStreakSegment` function. My focus has shifted to the diagonal streak check, specifically lines 60-65 in `modelExpander.ts`. I realize the checks for large `dx` and `dy` values can be too broad. I\'m modifying the logic to more precisely identify segments that represent streaks across different regions, like viewports, using the `len` and `maxDiag` variables. I\'m testing a more refined check, looking for how the machine coordinate data impacts these.\n\n\n**Addressing Flickering Issue**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on the screen flickering issue. I\'ve re-examined the cause in `App.tsx` and `CADCanvas.tsx` to understand the exact source. I realize that the `onUpdateCamera` prop\'s changing identity triggers an unnecessary re-render of `CADCanvas`. This leads to the canvas width and height being reset with `handleResize()`, which is what caused the flicker. I\'ll focus on stabilizing the `onUpdateCamera` prop, so the canvas is only redrawn as required.\n\n\n**Stabilizing Canvas Renders**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the flickering issue, realizing that setting `canvas.height` or `canvas.width` directly on the `<canvas>` DOM element clears the canvas, causing constant blanking during mouse or touch movement. I\'m focusing on fixing this by preventing unnecessary re-renders in `CADCanvas.tsx`. My current focus is to ensure the resize observer only mounts once and that width and height are only assigned if they have changed. Additionally, I need to wrap `handleUpdateCamera` in `App.tsx` with `useCallback` to prevent constant prop identity changes.\n\n\n"
