    thinking: "**Analyzing SPLINE Fixes**\n\nI\'m impressed by the elegant SPLINE fix in DXF; it\'s clean and precise. I\'m now cross-referencing this with the `libreDwgLoader.ts` file, specifically examining lines 608-625 to understand the implementation details and ensure consistency. The goal is to verify the fix within the broader context of the loading process.\n\n\n**Refining LWPOLYLINE Logic**\n\nI\'ve been drilling into the LWPOLYLINE logic within the context of SPLINE handling. Specifically, I\'m now scrutinizing the point filtering process, which is designed to identify and exclude anomalous tangent or origin points. I\'ve been focusing on how this is handled, and testing against edge cases.\n\n\n**Evaluating Point Filtering**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the point filtering mechanism for LWPOLYLINE, specifically the logic around handling potential artifact streaks. The code segment examines distance thresholds relative to origin points, and I\'m assessing its effectiveness in identifying and removing problematic tangent vectors or zero-length points, which would immediately strip the stray tangents. I\'m cross-checking this functionality in `modelExpander.ts`.\n\n\n**Assessing Segment Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the segment filtering logic within the LWPOLYLINE process. I\'m focusing on the `rejectSegment` function, specifically how it assesses segment length and potential origins or tangents. I\'m scrutinizing the conditions that flag a segment as problematic, like checking distance thresholds relative to the origin and detecting long diagonals or extremely long segments. I\'m focused on how this logic prevents the drawing of artifact streaks.\n\n\n**Examining Segment Filtering**\n\nI\'ve discovered the precise segment rejection criteria! Any segment to (0,0) longer than 300 units is eliminated. Diagonal segments exceeding length 500 with dx/dy both > 80 are dropped, too, as are non-axis-aligned segments longer than 1200. I also verified the artifact streak check in `modelExpander.ts`, particularly how it splits and removes streaks, and how it handles closed LWPOLYLINE entities. I\'m now exploring the camera updates in `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Implementing Camera Control**\n\nI\'ve defined `handleUpdateCamera` using `useCallback` to manage camera updates, passing it as `onUpdateCamera`. I\'m integrating this function into `src/components/CADCanvas.tsx`, specifically lines 990-1065, to handle window and container resize adjustments. I\'m focusing on ensuring integer dimensions update the canvas correctly to prevent screen flickering, and I\'m updating canvas dimensions on resize events.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Logic**\n\nI\'m now incorporating logic within `CADCanvas.tsx` to handle changes to viewport dimensions, and prevent screen flickering. I\'m focusing on lines 990-1065, specifically how integer dimensions update the canvas. I\'m updating the canvas dimensions on resize events, and assessing how the camera auto-adjusts when the aspect ratio changes, triggered by landscape/portrait mode switching.\n\n\n**Optimizing Camera Updates**\n\nI\'ve just validated the final camera implementation! The dependency array is correctly `[]`, preventing re-runs on camera changes. I removed the `visualViewport.addEventListener(\'resize\')` due to mobile drag/scroll issues, and I ensure the canvas dimensions are only modified when they actually differ, preventing flickering. It is now stable.\n\n\n"
