  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Loading Behavior**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the issue where large files either fail to load or display incomplete content. My investigation has led me to `modelExpander.ts`. I\'m specifically examining the `MAX_ENTITIES` constant, which is defined on line 100. My hope is to understand how this limit impacts loading and rendering for large files.\n\n\n**Analyzing Block Expansion Logic**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to how `modelExpander.ts` handles block expansion, particularly lines 110. The condition `expanded.length >= MAX_ENTITIES` caught my eye. Simultaneously, I\'ve re-examined `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically lines 730-740. I\'m now exploring the likelihood that `db.entities` may be empty in larger DWG files (or modern ACAD files), with the core model entities residing within `db.tables.BLOCK_RECORD.entries` under `*MODEL_SPACE`. This may suggest that we are never expanding past the first entity when this occurs, making large files impossible to load.\n\n\n**Refining Data Extraction**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the data extraction in `libreDwgLoader.ts`, specifically lines 695-697 and 730-740. It seems the code skips `modelSpaceFallback` when `db.entities` has *any* content, even a tiny bit, leaving most of the drawing data within the `*MODEL_SPACE` block completely untouched. It appears this omission results in a critical data loading failure for large files.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Entity Inclusion**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical insight: for AutoCAD DWG files, the core model data resides within the `*MODEL_SPACE` block, a distinction I previously missed. It appears `db.entities` might be empty, or contain only a small number of entities, and this prevents the use of `modelSpaceFallback`. Now, my focus is on ensuring `modelSpaceFallback` is used (or merged), by using the logic: if `entities.length === 0` OR `modelSpaceFallback.length > entities.length`, then use `modelSpaceFallback`. This should address the data loading failure.\n\n\n"
