  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing DXF Parsing**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the DXF parsing logic, specifically lines 139-148 in `src/lib/dxfParser.ts`. I\'m examining how the raw text data is processed, particularly the newline handling and the initial splitting into code-value pairs. My primary interest lies in understanding how this initial parsing stage sets the groundwork for subsequent data extraction and interpretation within the DXF structure.\n\n\n**Addressing Memory Consumption**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a critical memory bottleneck in the DXF parsing process. The current implementation creates multiple large string copies and allocates a massive array of string objects when handling large DXF files. On mobile devices with limited memory, this leads to frequent out-of-memory errors and tab crashes. I\'m exploring alternative parsing strategies, such as index scanning or regex-based approaches, to drastically reduce memory overhead.\n\n\n**Investigating File Handling**\n\nI\'m now diving into `libreDwgLoader.ts` to understand file ingestion. Specifically, I\'m tracing the data flow from file input to the LibreDWG engine, focusing on how `arrayBuffer` is passed to `dwg_read_data` in line 768. I\'m also examining the temporary file write via `wasm.FS.writeFile` and the subsequent read using `wasm.dwg_read_file`, looking into how the `db` variable gets populated, especially the `entities` array.\n\n\n**Validating Object Loading**\n\nI\'ve uncovered a critical flaw in how large DXF files are handled, specifically when entities are defined in the *MODEL_SPACE. If a layout viewport is present, the parser incorrectly discards the actual model space entities. This happens because the `db.entities` array, containing layout boundaries, incorrectly triggers a condition that results in the omission of the true machine or part entities. Consequently, large drawings are not displayed correctly. I\'m focusing on validating this and adjusting to always properly load the model space data.\n\n\n**Refining Model Space Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic to handle model space data. If `modelSpaceFallback` exists, it should become the primary model space drawing. I\'m also ensuring that entities from `db.entities` that are not duplicates or paper-space viewports are merged. The code on lines 695-703 is under review in light of this change. I\'m also investigating block handling, specifically how `modelSpaceFallback` affects the `blocks` array and how we handle entity merges using sets to track IDs to avoid duplicates.\n\n\n"
