    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Structure**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `dxfParser.ts` code, specifically focusing on how it handles different entity types. I\'ve noted the \'LWPOLYLINE\' and \'LEADER\' entities, and the conditional logic around them. I am paying close attention to the way the \'closed\' property is assigned based on the value of code 70, which is crucial for determining how each is ultimately parsed.\n\n\n**Investigating DXF Entity Parsing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the parsing of `LEADER` entities and their relation to `MULTILEADER` and `POLYLINE`. Specifically, I realized that the existing code, while handling `LEADER` via vertex parsing (10, 20, 42), appears to neglect the newer `MULTILEADER` type altogether. Further inspection reveals `POLYLINE` is handled separately, which might be fine, but I need to understand the implications of this approach. I will try to consider the implications of how these entity types relate and if there is overlap in the data contained within them.\n\n\n**Exploring LWPOLYLINE Parsing**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into how `LWPOLYLINE` vertex parsing is handled. I noticed the loop structure that extracts vertex data (x, y, bulge) and the crucial check for the `isSplineFrameVertex` flag, determined by code 70 and bitwise operations. This is important to ensure I understand how the code handles different vertex types, specifically the logic for disregarding spline frame vertices. I also need to understand the role of `SEQEND` within the parsing logic and it\'s relationship with `LWPOLYLINE` and when the loops break.\n\n\n**Clarifying POLYLINE Flag Parsing**\n\nI\'m focusing now on `POLYLINE` entity flag parsing, specifically group code 70. I realized bits 16 and 64 represent critical mesh types \342\200\223 polygon and polyface meshes respectively. These MUST be handled distinctly. My code will now detect when bit 64 or 16 are set to ensure appropriate vertex handling as mesh indices, not 2D coordinates. I need to make sure to accommodate this in both the `POLYLINE` entity, as well as the related `VERTEX` entity logic. This is an important consideration.\n\n\n**Uncovering Mesh Vertex Issue**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery: I realize how polyface mesh vertices corrupt the drawing! I\'ve confirmed that face indices within `POLYLINE` entities are mistakenly interpreted as 2D coordinates. This leads to phantom diagonal lines when parsing 3D parts and CAD symbols. This happens in the `VERTEX` entity where specific vertex flags are set. The `(vtxFlag & 128)` for polyface mesh vertices and `(vtxFlag & 64)` for face indices are the culprits, and must now be dealt with properly!\n\n\n**Addressing Mesh Face Misinterpretations**\n\nI\'ve clarified the issue related to polyface and 3D polygon mesh vertices. I realize that in the `VERTEX` entity, any vertex having `(flag & 128) !== 0` indicates a polyface mesh definition. More importantly, when the parent `POLYLINE` has `(polyFlag & 64) !== 0`, any vertex with `(flag & 128) === 0` is a face record. Parsing this as coordinates causes unwanted diagonal lines. I\'m moving onto the `SPLINE` entity in `libreDwgLoader.ts` and `dxfParser.ts` to see how those are implemented and to examine the interaction of control points and the closed flag.\n\n\n**Refining Spline Entity Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `SPLINE` entity parsing and I\'ve identified crucial group codes (12, 22, 32, 13, 23, 33) that represent tangent vectors, NOT coordinates, and should never be misinterpreted as such. I am also making sure knot values, weights, the spline degree and count of control points/fit points are handled properly. This is crucial for accurate spline representation.\n\n\n\n**Isolating Mesh Data**\n\nI\'ve determined I need to handle polyface mesh face records differently from vertices. Specifically, in `POLYLINE` entities, I\'ll check flags for polyface mesh (`& 64`) and polygon mesh (`& 16`). Face records will be ignored. I will also make sure to filter out `DEFPOINTS` and dimension block data to avoid phantom lines, and I now intend to ignore tangent vectors from `SPLINE` entities and isolate knot vector and fit points.\n\n\n**Merging Model Space Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the contents of the `*MODEL_SPACE` block in DWG files. I\'ve realized that the current logic in `libreDwgLoader.ts` was neglecting crucial model data present within the `db.tables.BLOCK_RECORD.entries[\'*MODEL_SPACE\']` structure. Consequently, many drawings appeared empty or incomplete. To resolve this, I\'m merging the entities from `*MODEL_SPACE` with the main `db.entities` to ensure that all drawing components are loaded and rendered.\n\nI now realize that the current strategy for handling model data neglects to integrate critical machine components stored within the `*MODEL_SPACE` block within DWG files. This oversight makes large files appear empty and must be corrected. I intend to merge the entities from `*MODEL_SPACE` with the primary `db.entities` to make sure all components are properly loaded.\n\n\n**Optimizing Memory Usage**\n\nI\'m now rewriting `parseDxfText` to avoid memory issues with large files. I\'m focusing on a line-by-line scanning method, using a lightweight regex or cursor scanner to prevent creating huge string arrays that cause mobile browser crashes. I am also investigating reasonable maximum limits in `modelExpander.ts` and ensuring that line segment length thresholds are handled intelligently, to prevent the deletion of real machine geometry and machine lines. I\'m prioritizing visible layers and refining line segment length limits. I am making sure the deletion of inclined or diagonal machine lines is avoided.\n\n\n"
