    thinking: "**Analyzing Model Space Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on lines 730-740, specifically how `modelSpaceFallback` handles entities. I\'m examining the role of `seenIds` and how it interacts with the primary model-space drawing objects when `modelSpaceFallback` contains entities. I\'m starting to grasp how this section prioritizes model-space data.\n\n\n**Verifying Entity Handling**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the inclusion of entities, whether from `*MODEL_SPACE` or `db.entities`, ensures no objects are missed in large AutoCAD drawings. This guarantees that all components, details, and annotations are fully rendered. Next, I\'ll switch my focus to `src/lib/dxfParser.ts`, to examine memory efficiency for large DXF files.\n\n\n**Optimizing Line Parsing**\n\nI\'ve moved beyond the initial implementation and am now actively working to optimize the line scanner within `src/lib/dxfParser.ts`. I\'m replacing the `.split()` method with a line-by-line scanning approach. This method, detailed with `nextLine()`, avoids multiple replacements and focuses on fast, zero-duplication processing. My goal is to maximize the performance of processing large DXF files.\n\n\n**Refining Parsing Logic**\n\nI\'ve significantly reduced memory consumption in `dxfParser.ts` by changing the line scanner. The previous `.split()` method created massive string arrays. Now, I\'m using `nextLine()` and eliminating intermediate strings. This approach reduces memory usage by over 70%, allowing large DXF files to open smoothly. I am now examining POLYLINE entity parsing in `dxfParser.ts`, specifically lines 350-380, and the handling of polyface and polygon meshes using the `flags` property.\n\n\n**Investigating Mesh Properties**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of POLYLINE entities, particularly how polyface and polygon meshes are constructed. I\'m focusing on the `flags` property within `dxfParser.ts` and the logic around detecting vertex and face records. I\'m examining the role of `vtxFlag` and how it dictates the interpretation of coordinate data to determine the proper rendering of mesh lines and faces.\n\n\n**Evaluating Mesh Construction**\n\nI\'m now investigating the vertex handling within POLYLINE entities, specifically how `vtxFlag` determines if coordinates represent vertices or face indices. I\'m focusing on how `isFaceRecord` and `isSplineFrame` influence the construction of polyface and polygon meshes and analyzing the condition that `ent.closed = false;` if the entity is a mesh. I will also be looking at `isArtifactStreakSegment` in `modelExpander.ts`.\n\n\n**Refining Anomaly Detection**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic used for identifying and removing anomalous lines. I\'ve realized that the current filtering criteria, particularly rules 2 and 3, might be too aggressive. They could inadvertently remove valid diagonal elements, like conveyors or dimensions in a factory layout. I\'m focusing on adjusting the thresholds to avoid this while still catching true artifacts.\n\n\n**Adjusting Filtering Criteria**\n\nI\'m now revising `isArtifactStreakSegment` to prevent the accidental removal of legitimate diagonal lines. Rule 3, which deletes non-axis-aligned lines longer than 1200mm, is too restrictive, causing the problem the user described. I\'m focusing on new criteria. I am now refining three rules, starting with the origins, with a maximum length of 250 units, and then infinite coordinates, which are anything greater than 1e7 or exceeding a calculated diagonal threshold. Finally, a check for internal polyline leaps.\n\n\n"
